Fluid antennas (FAs), also known as movable antennas (MAs), provide a promising solution to this issue~\cite{10286328, 10909643}. Unlike conventional fixed-position antennas (FPAs), FAs can reconfigure the positions of radiating elements within a bounded aperture, thereby changing the effective wireless channel~\cite{10654366}. 
As illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:1}, an FA-enabled UAV communication system exploits two forms of mobility in a LAWN: the macro-mobility of the UAV trajectory and the micro-mobility of FA position reconfiguration. 
Their complementary integration is defined as dual mobility. UAV macro-mobility adjusts the large-scale A2G propagation geometry, including the link distance, elevation angle, and blockage condition, whereas FA micro-mobility selects favorable local channel responses through antenna-position reconfiguration. Operating over different spatial and temporal scales, these two forms of mobility are complementary and motivate the joint design of UAV trajectory, FA positions, and beamforming.
\footnote{This article focuses on UAV-mounted FAs, where trajectory mobility is combined with local antenna-position reconfiguration. FAs may also be deployed at BSs, vehicles, or other ground terminals, but such architectures involve different hardware constraints and optimization variables.}
Introducing FA positions as optimization variables transforms the conventional joint design of UAV trajectory and beamforming into a multi-variable optimization problem involving UAV trajectory, FA positions, and beamforming. Their strong coupling, together with UAV mobility limits, FA reconfiguration constraints, power and energy budgets, and security requirements, makes the joint design computationally challenging.
Moreover, slowly varying prior information, such as environment maps and user mobility patterns, and rapidly varying real-time information, such as instantaneous channel state information (CSI) and UAV state, are available over different timescales, introducing uncertainty and requiring efficient offline-online coordination.
Motivated by these observations, this article presents a system-level perspective on FA-enabled UAV communication with dual mobility as the central organizing principle. The main contributions are threefold.
First, we explain the advantages of introducing FAs into UAV communications in terms of onboard resource constraints, response lag compensation, attitude perturbation mitigation, multi-user interference management, and mission-oriented scenario adaptation.
Second, we identify the key design challenges arising from the coupling among UAV trajectory, FA positions, and beamforming, and propose a hybrid offline-online optimization framework that exploits prior information for long-term planning and real-time information for fast online adaptation. A case study on FA-enabled UAV-assisted vehicular communications demonstrates the effectiveness of this framework.
Third, we discuss promising research directions, including integrated sensing and communication (ISAC), secure communication, mobile edge computing (MEC), and aerial relaying.
\section{Fundamentals of FA-Enabled UAV Communications}
\subsection{Motivation for Introducing FAs into UAV Communications}
Building on the dual mobility introduced in Section I, we explain why FAs are particularly suitable for UAV communication systems. The key observation is that UAV trajectory planning and FA position reconfiguration operate on complementary spatial and temporal scales~\cite{11214460}. UAV trajectory planning primarily adjusts the large-scale propagation geometry, including the A2G link distance, elevation angle, and blockage condition, whereas FA position reconfiguration enables fine-grained local channel adaptation within the antenna aperture. Thus, FAs complement UAV mobility by providing a faster physical-layer adaptation capability.
This complementarity is particularly valuable in LAWNs, where UAV trajectory planning is constrained by flight dynamics, energy budgets, no-fly zones, collision avoidance, and mission duration, and therefore cannot respond rapidly to local channel variations.
In contrast, FAs can select favorable antenna positions to mitigate small-scale fading, compensate for attitude-induced channel perturbations, and reshape multi-user channel correlations~\cite{11119811}. 
These capabilities make FAs well suited to dynamic UAV communication environments.
\subsection{Advantages of FA-Enabled UAV Communication Systems}
\subsubsection{Onboard Resource Constraints}
FAs are well suited to UAV platforms with limited onboard resources. 
Conventional multi-antenna arrays achieve array, diversity, and spatial multiplexing gains by increasing the number of antenna elements and RF chains~\cite{10354003}. 
However, this approach is difficult to implement on UAVs because of payload weight and power consumption constraints.
By selecting favorable antenna positions, FAs provide additional spatial selectivity with moderate hardware complexity, offering a lightweight and reconfigurable means of exploiting spatial diversity.
\subsubsection{Response Lag Compensation}
UAV trajectory planning is governed by flight dynamics and therefore responds much more slowly than that of small-scale channel fluctuations, whereas FA position reconfiguration can operate on a shorter timescale. 
FAs can thus improve instantaneous link quality even when the UAV is hovering, following a prescribed trajectory, or restricted to a safe flight corridor~\cite{11271535}. 
This capability enables fast local channel adaptation without frequent UAV maneuvering.
\subsubsection{Attitude Perturbation Mitigation}
FAs can mitigate the impact of UAV attitude perturbations. Rotor vibration, wind gusts, maneuvering, and attitude-control errors can cause short-term variations in antenna position, polarization, phase relationships, and link geometry. With FPAs, these perturbations directly translate into equivalent-channel fluctuations, whereas FAs can adapt their positions to compensate for such variations. This position-domain adaptation can reduce received power fluctuations and coherent-combining losses, thereby improving link reliability and service continuity.
\subsubsection{Multi-User Interference Management}
FAs also provide an additional DoF for multi-user interference management~\cite{11302793}. 
Conventional beamforming optimizes transmit weights under fixed antenna positions, whereas FAs additionally allow the effective channel response to be reconfigured. Different FA positions can induce different channel correlations and interference coupling patterns, thereby changing the feasible beamforming space. Joint optimization of FA positions and beamforming can therefore improve both desired-signal enhancement and inter-user interference suppression.
\subsubsection{Mission-Oriented Scenario Adaptation}
FAs are also attractive for mission-oriented applications such as temporary coverage, emergency communications, aerial relaying, and IoT data collection~\cite{11240557}. These scenarios often involve rapid deployment, dynamic network topologies, and incomplete infrastructure. 
By enabling fine-grained local channel reconfiguration, FAs allow UAVs to adapt to changing user distributions, blockage conditions, and service requirements without frequent trajectory adjustments. 
This capability is particularly beneficial when UAVs operate under limited energy budgets, restricted flight paths, or steady-hovering requirements.
