\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:SCIENTIFIC-BACKGROUND}
Thyroid cancer is one of the most prevalent endocrine malignancies, with an increasing incidence worldwide~\cite{biopsies_rate}. 
Diagnosis relies primarily on ultrasound (US) imaging, which is used to assess nodule characteristics and guide biopsy decisions. However, current practice leads to a high rate of unnecessary biopsies, with at least half of those performed proving to be benign~\cite{biopsies_rate}. Existing risk stratification systems such as ACR TI-RADS~\cite{ti_rads} rely on qualitative visual assessment, introducing interobserver variability and limiting diagnostic reliability.
Radiomics~\cite{radiomics} has emerged as a promising complementary 
approach, enabling operator-independent extraction of quantitative 
imaging features from US images. Combined with machine learning classifiers, radiomics-based models have shown competitive diagnostic performance for thyroid nodule classification~\cite{cleere2022}, with tools such as PyRadiomics~\cite{pyradiomics} providing standardized and reproducible feature extraction in this domain~\cite{salmanpour}.
However, two critical limitations persist in existing approaches: limited interpretability, and the absence of predictive uncertainty quantification, both essential for clinical adoption.
To address these limitations, we propose a framework that 
evaluates multiple machine learning classifiers on radiomic features 
extracted via PyRadiomics from the publicly available 
TN5000 dataset~\cite{tn5000}, selecting the model that best balances 
discriminative performance and interpretability. We further integrate 
Cross-Conformal Prediction with label-conditional coverage 
guarantees to provide formal uncertainty quantification~\cite{vovk2015}, 
allowing the model to explicitly flag diagnostically uncertain cases 
rather than forcing a hard binary classification. 
\section{Data and Methods}
\label{sec:DATA-AND-METHODS}
\subsection{TN5000 Dataset}
All experiments were conducted on TN5000~\cite{tn5000}, a publicly 
available dataset comprising 5000 US images of thyroid 
nodules, annotated with biopsy-confirmed labels and bounding box coordinates by expert radiologists.
Focusing exclusively on classification, images were cropped to the bounding box annotations prior to feature extraction, restricting radiomic feature computation to the nodule 
region of interest (ROI), as  shown in~\autoref{fig:sample_pair}.
