We adopted the official training/validation/test split provided with the 
dataset, yielding 3500 training, 500 validation, and 1000 test images, 
with approximately 71\
training set, which was taken into account during model training.
\subsection{Radiomic Feature Extraction}
Radiomic features were extracted from each nodule ROI using 
PyRadiomics v3.1.0~\cite{pyradiomics}, an open-source Python 
library for standardized radiomic feature computation.
Since all images are two-dimensional US scans, extraction was configured in 2D mode with prior intensity normalization and a bin width of 25 for discretization, consistent with the PyRadiomics default.
A total of 102 features were extracted 
from seven feature families: First Order Statistics (18), 
Shape-based 2D (9), Gray Level Co-occurrence Matrix (GLCM, 24), 
Gray Level Run Length Matrix (GLRLM, 16), Gray Level Size Zone 
Matrix (GLSZM, 16), Neighbouring Gray Tone Difference Matrix 
(NGTDM, 5), and Gray Level Dependence Matrix (GLDM, 14).
\subsection{Feature Selection and Standardization}
Given the well-known redundancy of radiomic features~\cite{correlation}, we applied a Spearman correlation-based filter, which captures monotonic relationships without assuming linearity.
For each pair of features with $|\rho| > 0.90$, one was removed~\cite{spearman}, yielding 36 features from five 
of the original seven families: First Order Statistics (12), GLCM (12), 
GLRLM (7), NGTDM (3), and Shape-based 2D (2). Features from the 
GLSZM and GLDM families were entirely removed, indicating high redundancy with the retained ones.
The retained features were then standardized using z-score normalization applied separately for each feature, with mean and standard deviation estimated on the training set and applied to validation and test sets.
\subsection{Classification}
Six classifiers were evaluated on the retained 36 features: Logistic Regression with LASSO penalization (LR-LASSO), 
Generalized Additive Model (GAM), Random Forest, Extra Trees, 
XGBoost, and a Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) with two hidden layers. All models were trained with class weights proportional to the 
inverse class frequency to address class imbalance, and subsequently 
compared on the validation set using the Area Under the ROC Curve (AUC), sensitivity, specificity, 
precision, negative predictive value (NPV), and F1-score.
\subsection{Label-Conditional Cross-Conformal Prediction}
To quantify predictive uncertainty, we applied Cross-Conformal 
Prediction (CCP)~\cite{vovk2015}, a framework that produces 
prediction sets with guaranteed coverage $1-\epsilon$ without 
distributional assumptions. 
CCP extends split conformal prediction via $K$-fold cross validation:
the classifier is trained $K$ times on $K-1$ folds, conformity scores are 
computed on the held-out fold, using the full training set as calibration set,  which in our case corresponds to the merged training and validation sets (4000 US images).
Following~\cite{vovk2015}, the computation of the conformity scores is based on the log-odds of the classifier output:
\begin{equation}
 \alpha = \left\{
\begin{array}{crl} 
\log\frac{\hat{P}(Y=1\mid X)}{\hat{P}(Y=0\mid X)} & if & y = 1 [6pt]
-\log\frac{\hat{P}(Y=1\mid X)}{\hat{P}(Y=0\mid X)} & if & y = 0
\end{array}
\right.
\end{equation}
Given the class imbalance in TN5000, we adopted the \textit{Label-Conditional} 
Cross-Conformal Prediction (CCCP),
which guarantees coverage separately for each class:
$P(y \in \Gamma^\epsilon \mid Y=y) \geq 1-\epsilon$. The prediction set $\Gamma^\epsilon$ for each 
test image is defined as:
\begin{equation}
\Gamma^{\epsilon} \coloneqq \{y \in \{0,1\} \mid p^y > \epsilon\},
\end{equation}
where the \textit{label-conditional} p-values $p^y$ are:
\begin{equation}
p^y = \frac{\sum_{k=1}^{K} \left|\left\{ i \in S_k \mid y_i = y,\; 
\alpha_{i,k} \leq \alpha^y_k \right\}\right| + 1}
{\sum_{k=1}^{K} \left|\left\{ i \in S_k \mid y_i = y 
\right\}\right| + 1},
\end{equation}
where $\alpha_{i,k}$ is the conformity score of calibration example $i$ 
in fold $S_k$, computed using the model trained on all remaining 
folds $S_{-k}$; $\alpha^y_k$ is the conformity score of the test 
example under assumed label $y$, computed using the same model. In our binary setting, this yields four possible prediction sets for each test image: certain 
benign $\{B\}$, certain malignant $\{M\}$, uncertain $\{B,M\}$, 
or empty $\{\}$. 
\section{Results}
\label{sec:RESULTS}
\subsection{Classifier Selection}
The performance metrics of the six classifiers on the validation set are reported in~\autoref{tab:RES_VALIDATION}.
All models achieved comparable AUC values ($\approx0.88-0.89$), consistent with the performance reported in the radiomics literature 
for thyroid nodule classification~\cite{cleere2022}. 
However, substantial differences emerged in sensitivity and specificity. Tree-based ensemble methods and 
MLP achieved high sensitivity (up to 0.95 for Extra Trees) and NPV at the 
cost of low specificity (as low as 0.51), indicating a tendency 
to over-predict the predominant class of malignancy despite class weighting. XGBoost partially mitigated this imbalance 
($0.72$ specificity), but remained inferior to LR-LASSO and GAM, which achieved the best sensitivity-specificity trade-off. 
