\subsection{Feature selection}
For each of the clinical data classifiers and combinations (A, B, C, AB, AC, BC, ABC) we conduct sequential feature selection (SFS)~\cite{FERRI1994403}.
This algorithm begins with a single feature and greedily selects a subset of features based on those which achieve the lowest average development set loss.
Typically, the best development set performance is achieved for a subset of the features, after which additional features degrade performance.
\subsection{Lung ultrasound classifier}
We employ the LUS classification architecture presented in~\cite{ultrai2025escmid}.
This architecture represents a multi-instance learning approach where the $N'$ scans from a single patient are denoted as the unordered set $\boldsymbol{X}$ ($\mathbb{R}^{(N' \times H \times W \times C)}$).
Each scan has input sizes of $H \times W$, which is always resized to 224$\ \times\ $224, and $C =\ $1 because input scans are greyscale.
Each scan in the set is passed through an augmentation layer which imposes brightness and contrast jitter and random translations and rotations~\cite{Shorten2019}.
Subsequently, mean and variance normalisation is applied and scans are fed separately through a feature extractor, which is either a ResNet-18 or a ResNet-50~\cite{He_2016_CVPR,Russakovsky2015}.
The output vectors from these ResNets are either 512 (for ResNet-18) or 2048 (for ResNet-50) dimensional, and are projected to $h_d=\ $128, 256, or 512 such that the TB classification layers maintain the same number of trainable parameters during hyperparameter optimisation.
An embedding, unique to each of the 14 anatomical scan locations, with the same dimensionality ($h_d$) is then added to the feature vectors from each respective site.
These $N'$ feature vectors pass through two linear layers.
The output of the first increases the dimensionality to four times that of the input (4$\ \cdot\ $$h_d$) and then applies a ReLU activation.
The second layer decreases the dimensionality of the vector back to $h_d$.
Subsequently, an additive style attention is applied, which aims to select the scans which are most useful for TB classification.
This converts the set of $N'$ features to a single feature vector~\cite{bahdanau2014neural}.
Finally, this single feature vector is passed through a two layer classifier head, which again increases and then decreases the dimensionality of the vectors, this time by a factor of two.
The output of the classifier is a scalar sigmoid approximating the posterior probability of TB.
This classifier is optimised over the hyperparameters listed in Table~\ref{tab:hyperparameters} in a grid-fashion. 
\subsection{Fusion of classifiers}
\label{subsec:exp_setup_fused_classifier}
\begin{figure}
 \centering
 \includegraphics[width=0.8\linewidth]{SATNAC_EXP_SETUP.drawio.pdf}
 \caption{Fusion of clinical data into ultrasound classifier through the concatenation of the encoded feature vector before the final classification layers (classification head).}
 \label{fig:fusion_classifier}
\end{figure}
We consider the fusion of the LUS and clinical data classifiers using three approaches.
First, the encoded feature vector of dimensionality ($h_f$) is concatenated with the feature vector obtained after the attention pooling layer, as illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:fusion_classifier}.
The result of this concatenation is then fed to the classification head as previously described.
Second, the average of the two output probabilities produced by the LUS and the clinical classifiers is considered.
Finally, LR is trained on the output logits of the two classifiers (before the sigmoid activations).
The final two alternative configurations are illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:fusion_alternative_exp_setup}.
This model is trained on the output scores produced for the development data (i.e. out-of-fold) by each classifier, again within a cross-validation scheme.
Out-of-fold scores are utilised as classifiers often produce overconfident scores on the training data which may negatively impact the fusion of the scores when applied to held-out data.
The application of LR in this way can be seen as computing a trained weighted average of the two classifier scores.
Preliminary experiments evaluating max/min score selection of the clinical and LUS models, thereby optimising sensitivity or specificity respectively, consistently underperformed the strategies presented here and are not described further.
Additionally, preliminary experiments showed that model calibration also did not lead to any improvement in development set performance.
