\section{Results}
Firstly, the performance of the baseline ResNet LUS classifier (Section~\ref{subsec:lus_classifier}) is reported.
Next, Section~\ref{subsec:clinical_classifier} presents the performance of classifiers using only the clinical and demographic data. 
Finally, Section~\ref{subsec:fused_classifier} reports the performance after fusion of the two classifiers.
\subsection{LUS classifier}
\label{subsec:lus_classifier}
A grid-style hyperparameter optimisation on the development sets yields the values listed in Table~\ref{tab:hyperparameters}.
The development and independent test AUROCs for the resulting model (Baseline LUS) are presented alongside the clinical classifier in Table~\ref{tab:clinical_classifier_results}.
A test set AUROC of 0.91 [0.86, 0.96] is achieved by the LUS-only model, which is comparable to the value reported in previous work~\cite{ultrai2025escmid}.
We note again that, in the results reported here, clinically diagnosed TB cases are counted as TB positive, while in previous work they were not.
This performance serves as reference for the clinical and fusion classifiers.
\subsection{Clinical classifier}
\label{subsec:clinical_classifier}
The classification results achieved when training either LR or the MLP on the availability classes defined in Table~\ref{tab:clinical_features} are presented in Table~\ref{tab:clinical_classifier_results}.
Interestingly, classification using only self-reported values (A) outperforms classification based on measured values (B).
This may be due to the critical symptoms captured in class A, such as fever, cough, dyspnoea, weight loss, and known HIV, which are all captured in the features identified to improve development set performance during feature selection.
In Table~\ref{tab:clinical_classifier_results}, it is also clear that classes A and B provide mutually beneficial information.
This is evident because the union of all self-reported and measured values (development AUROC for AB after SFS is 0.87\ [0.83,0.90] for MLP) 
improves performance over training on either A or B alone (A: 0.82\ [0.78,0.86], or B:0.82\ [0.78,0.86]).
Leveraging all available collected values (ABC) and conducting feature selection results in the best overall development (0.89 [0.85,0.92]) and test-set performance (0.89 [0.83,0.96]), suggesting that although the POC tests considered here do not show great TB discrimination on their own (dev C: 0.57 [0.53, 0.61] for MLP) the information remains mutually beneficial relative to classes A and B.
It is important to note, however, that if the efficiency of the screening system is paramount, and the conduction of finger-prick based POC tests presents a barrier to screening, then a system which achieves only marginally worse performance using only self-reported and easily measurable values (AB) can still be trained.
