\subsubsection{Feature selection}
The best development AUROC is jointly achieved by LR and the MLP after SFS using all availability classes.
The 18 selected features are, in order of importance:
BMI, heart rate, hypertension, recent antibiotics, previous TB, weight loss, general state, days of cough, sex, malaria RDT, fever, night sweats, dyspnoea, HIV, pitting oedema, malaria RDT not done, age, and known HIV status.
Interestingly, two of the W4SS symptoms~\cite{world2021consolidated} (fever, night sweats) appear less informative for the clinical classifier than features such as heart rate, hypertension, antibiotics, which are not generally associated with TB screening.
When SFS is applied to the subset of features which are measured and self-reported (AB), the most to least informative were: BMI, heart rate, recent antibiotics, hypertension, previous TB, weight loss, general state, days of cough, sex, respiratory rate, night sweats, age, fever, and dyspnoea.
Except for respiratory rate, the features selected from the classes A and B are also selected from the pool of all features (ABC).
These selected features are all easy to capture, and reduce the number of necessary inputs by 27 values compared to a naive capture-all approach.
\subsection{Fused classifier}
\label{subsec:fused_classifier}
During fusion, only a subset of the hyperparameters is swept again, namely: whether the feature extractor is frozen, the hidden dimensionality $h_d$, and the number of training epochs.
The remaining hyperparameters are fixed to those which afforded the lowest average development set loss for the LUS-only classifier (Table~\ref{tab:hyperparameters}). 
This sweep is repeated independently for each availability class subset.
As the training of the LUS classifier is substantially more computationally intensive than the training of the clinical classifier, we do not repeat feature selection, but train the fused classifier either using the full feature vector or the subset of features identified during SFS with the MLP. 
Additionally, we do not further consider in isolation the subset of variables which require a POC test (C) or the combination of the self-reported/measured values and the POC testing class (AC,BC) as these were outperformed on the development sets by the more easily obtained classes A and B.
The results for all fusion experiments are presented in Table~\ref{tab:fusion_results}.
