\begin{abstract}
Thyroid ultrasound supports the assessment of nodule malignancy, but classification predictions often lack evidence linked to familiar clinical descriptions. Radiomics provides explicitly defined measurements that can support such explanations through a clinical dictionary. However, using these measurements as interpretable evidence requires reliable estimation from ultrasound images and preservation of measurement fidelity when optimizing classification performance. We propose a clinical-dictionary radiomic bottleneck model for thyroid nodule classification. Within this model, Family-Aware Radiomic Learning (FARL) estimates measurements with balanced supervision across feature families. Fidelity-Guided Bottleneck Modeling (FGBM) constructs a fixed bottleneck, followed by joint optimization of measurement estimation and classification under fidelity guidance. Decision evidence decomposition then traces feature contributions through a linear classifier and organizes them by clinical dictionary categories. Experiments on ThyroidXL and TN3K show competitive classification performance, while case studies illustrate feature contributions for benign and malignant nodules. These findings indicate interpretable classification with inspectable quantitative evidence informed by clinical knowledge. \href{https://github.com/Lsyyh/Learning-to-Measure-and-Diagnose}{Code is available online}.
\end{abstract}
\keywords{thyroid ultrasound; concept bottleneck model; radiomics; clinical dictionary}
\section{Introduction}
Clinicians rely on ultrasound findings to determine which thyroid nodules warrant biopsy or follow-up and which require no further evaluation~\cite{acrtirads}. Classification models can support these decisions, but a predicted label alone does not reveal which findings support the prediction. For these predictions to be clinically interpretable, their underlying evidence should be inspectable and linked to familiar ultrasound descriptions.
Existing studies have explored interpretable concepts and visual prototypes to explain image classification decisions. Concept Bottleneck Models (CBMs) map images to human-interpretable concepts and use the estimated concept values for classification~\cite{lfcbm,pcbm,adacbm}. Prototype-based methods instead relate image regions to learned prototypes or part-based concepts, providing visual evidence for their predictions~\cite{cipl,pcmnet}. However, the resulting explanations do not necessarily align with established ultrasound descriptors, limiting how directly they can be interpreted in clinical assessment.
To connect model explanations more closely with clinical assessment, radiomics provides quantitative measurements of lesion geometry, intensity, and texture, computed within segmented regions~\cite{pyradiomics}. Beyond their direct use for classification~\cite{guerrisi2024,fang2025}, these measurements can also support clinical interpretation. A clinical dictionary links selected measurements to five familiar ultrasound descriptor categories: composition, echogenicity, shape, margin, and echogenic foci~\cite{tu10}. This association provides clinical context, while explicit measurement definitions allow predicted values to be checked against measured references. However, directly using these measurements for classification requires segmentation at inference. Radiomic measurements have also been used as additional supervision during model training~\cite{rlar}, but this does not make them the primary basis of model decisions. This motivates predicting clinically associated radiomic measurements from ultrasound images and using these predictions for classification.
Using predicted, clinically associated radiomic measurements to perform image-only classification raises two interrelated challenges. First, radiomic measurements span different families that capture complementary aspects of nodule characteristics. These families may differ in learning difficulty, so improved overall estimation accuracy does not guarantee reliable predictions across them. Second, the predicted measurements must support benign--malignant classification while remaining faithful to measured references. Classification performance and measurement fidelity are not necessarily aligned, as errors in individual measurements can affect classification differently. Reducing overall estimation error may not improve diagnostic discrimination, whereas optimizing classification alone may compromise measurement fidelity.
To address these challenges, we propose a clinical-dictionary radiomic bottleneck model for thyroid nodule classification. The model estimates quantitative measurements directly from ultrasound images and performs classification without requiring segmentation at inference. Our main contributions are threefold: \textbf{(1) Family-Aware Radiomic Learning (FARL)} balances supervision across radiomic measurement families to learn complementary quantitative information. \textbf{(2) Fidelity-Guided Bottleneck Modeling (FGBM)} builds on these estimates, using measurement fidelity to guide bottleneck construction, after which it jointly optimizes estimation and classification to limit measurement drift. \textbf{(3) Decision evidence decomposition} enables feature contributions to be traced and organized by clinical dictionary categories, with a linear classifier making decisions solely from selected measurements. Experiments on two thyroid ultrasound datasets, ThyroidXL and TN3K, demonstrate competitive classification performance, while case-level analyses illustrate feature contributions for benign and malignant nodules.
