\section{Method}
\subsection{Model Overview}
Figure~\ref{fig:overview} illustrates our model, which predicts radiomic measurements from ultrasound images and uses selected estimates as a diagnostic bottleneck. For each case, let $X=\{x_1,\ldots,x_n\}$ denote its $n$ ultrasound images and $y$ denote its label. Using the corresponding lesion masks, a reference vector $\mathbf r_t\in\mathbb{R}^{p}$ comprising $p$ radiomic features with explicit measurement definitions is extracted offline from each image $x_t$. The clinical dictionary associates selected features with clinical description categories, where $D_k$ denotes the features associated with category $k$. FARL learns to estimate the full radiomic feature vector from images, while FGBM constructs a fixed bottleneck $M\subseteq\bigcup_k D_k$ and jointly optimizes feature estimation and classification. Feature contributions from a linear classifier using only the selected predictions are traced and organized by clinical dictionary categories.
\subsection{Family-Aware Radiomic Learning}
FARL learns radiomic feature estimation from offline measurements. To reduce target skewness and scale differences, we apply a feature-wise rank-Gaussian transformation $T$ fitted on training data. The encoder $f_\theta$ predicts transformed features, which are inverse-transformed and averaged to obtain case-level estimates:
\begin{equation}
\begin{gathered}
\mathbf z_t=T(\mathbf r_t),\quad
\hat{\mathbf z}_t=f_\theta(x_t),\quad
\hat{\mathbf r}(X)=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{t=1}^{n}T^{-1}(\hat{\mathbf z}_t).
\end{gathered}
\label{eq:measure}
\end{equation}
Radiomic feature families differ in the number of features and their learning characteristics. To prevent larger families from dominating supervision, FARL averages SmoothL1 ($\ell_{\mathrm{SL1}}$) errors within each family and weights the family losses equally:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal L_{\mathrm{FARL}}=\mathbb E_X\Bigg[
\frac{1}{n|\mathcal F|}
\sum_{t=1}^{n}\sum_{g\in\mathcal F}\
\frac{1}{|g|}\sum_{j\in g}
\ell_{\mathrm{SL1}}(\hat z_{tj},z_{tj})
\Bigg].
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:far}
\end{equation}
Here, $\mathcal F$ denotes the geometry, intensity, and texture feature families, where \(g\in\mathcal F\) indexes a family. The terms $z_{tj}$ and $\hat z_{tj}$ denote the $j$-th components of the corresponding vectors, while $\mathbb E_X$ denotes averaging over cases.
\subsection{Fidelity-Guided Bottleneck Modeling}
Building on FARL, FGBM constructs a fixed bottleneck from clinically associated radiomic features. Subsequently, it jointly optimizes feature estimation and classification under fidelity guidance.
\textbf{Bottleneck construction.} From candidates with clinical dictionary support, we retain features within each category based on reliability and redundancy. Using case-level out-of-fold predictions and corresponding reference measurements from the training data, reliability $\rho_j$ measures the Spearman correlation between predicted and reference values of feature $j$, while redundancy $R_{jl}$ measures the absolute Spearman correlation between predicted features $j$ and $l$. For each dictionary category $k$, candidates in $D_k$ are ranked by reliability. Starting with $S_k=\varnothing$, a candidate $j\in D_k$ is retained according to:
\begin{equation}
\begin{gathered}
\rho_j\geq\tau_{\mathrm{rel}},\
R_{jl}\leq\tau_{\mathrm{red}} (\forall l\in S_k) \
\Longrightarrow S_k\leftarrow S_k\cup\{j\}.
\end{gathered}
\label{eq:select}
\end{equation}
Here, $\tau_{\mathrm{rel}}$ and $\tau_{\mathrm{red}}$ denote reliability and redundancy thresholds. The final bottleneck $M=\bigcup_k S_k$ combines selected members across categories and remains fixed during subsequent optimization.
\textbf{Joint optimization.} With the selected members, FGBM jointly optimizes radiomic feature estimation and classification. For each $j\in M$, the case-level estimates are standardized and passed to a linear classifier:
\begin{equation}
\begin{gathered}
u_j(X)=\frac{\hat r_j(X)-\mu_j}{s_j},\
\eta(X)=b+\sum_{j\in M}\beta_j u_j(X).
\end{gathered}
\label{eq:head}
\end{equation}
Here, $u_j(X)$ denotes the standardized case-level estimate of feature $j$, with fixed standardization parameters $\mu_j$ and $s_j$. The coefficients $\beta_j$ and bias $b$ define the classifier, whose logit $\eta(X)$ yields the malignancy probability $\sigma(\eta(X))$.
To constrain classification-driven measurement drift, we introduce a fidelity loss relative to the frozen FARL predictions $\hat{\mathbf z}^{(0)}_t=f_{\theta_0}(x_t)$. The fixed weights $a_j$ are initialized as $1+\rho_j$ and doubled for dictionary-supported features to prioritize their fidelity preservation. They are then normalized to mean one within each radiomic family:
\begin{equation}
\begin{gathered}
\mathcal L_{\mathrm{fid}}=\mathbb E_X\Bigg[
\frac{1}{n|\mathcal F|}
\sum_{t=1}^{n}\sum_{g\in\mathcal F}\
\frac{1}{|g|}\sum_{j\in g}
a_j\ell_{\mathrm{SL1}}(\hat z_{tj},\hat z^{(0)}_{tj})
\Bigg],
\mathcal L_{\mathrm{FGBM}}=\mathcal L_{\mathrm{FARL}}
+\lambda_{\mathrm{cls}}\mathcal L_{\mathrm{cls}}
+\lambda_{\mathrm{fid}}\mathcal L_{\mathrm{fid}}.
\end{gathered}
\label{eq:fid_joint}
\end{equation}
Here, $\mathcal L_{\mathrm{cls}}$ is case-level binary cross-entropy, and $\lambda_{\mathrm{cls}},\lambda_{\mathrm{fid}}$ weight classification and fidelity. The measurement loss maintains supervision against reference values, while the fidelity loss constrains deviations from frozen Stage 1 predictions. Both losses cover the full radiomic feature vector to keep joint optimization anchored to the original measurement learning objective.
\subsection{Decision Evidence Decomposition}
The decision score is decomposed into contributions from selected radiomic features through the linear classifier. Let $G_j=\{k:j\in D_k\}$ denote the clinical dictionary categories associated with feature $j$. For features associated with multiple categories, their contributions are distributed equally:
\begin{equation}
\begin{gathered}
c_j(X)=\beta_j u_j(X),\quad\
C_k(X)=\sum_{j\in M:\,k\in G_j}\frac{c_j(X)}{|G_j|},
\eta(X)=b+\sum_k C_k(X).
\end{gathered}
\label{eq:evidence}
\end{equation}
This decomposition exactly reconstructs the decision logit, with category contributions organizing diagnostic evidence rather than representing independent predictions of clinical ultrasound findings.
\section{Experiments and Results}
\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\textbf{Dataset.} We evaluate our method on two public thyroid ultrasound datasets, ThyroidXL~\cite{thyroidxl} and TN3K~\cite{tn3k}, containing 11,554 and 3,493 image--mask pairs, respectively. Benign--malignant labels are obtained from cytology or histopathology for ThyroidXL and from TNCD~\cite{tncd} for TN3K. We use fixed training, validation, and test splits comprising 2,678/670/736 patients for ThyroidXL and 2,303/576/614 images for TN3K. Evaluation is patient-level for ThyroidXL and image-level for TN3K, per the dataset protocols.
\textbf{Implementation Details.} We use PyRadiomics to extract 102 radiomic features from native-resolution images using lesion masks, covering geometry (9), intensity (18), and texture (75). We adopt ImageNet-pretrained ConvNeXt-Tiny with $384\times384$ inputs and AdamW (learning rate $10^{-4}$, weight decay $5\times10^{-4}$, batch size 8). Training consists of 80 FARL epochs followed by 20 FGBM epochs, with $\lambda_{\mathrm{cls}}=0.5$ and $\lambda_{\mathrm{fid}}=3$. Bottleneck selection uses $\tau_{\mathrm{rel}}=0.3$ and $\tau_{\mathrm{red}}=0.9$. Checkpoints in both stages are selected by the highest mean validation $R^2$ across 102 transformed radiomic targets. Experiments are conducted in PyTorch on an NVIDIA RTX 4090 GPU and repeated with three random seeds. We report mean $\pm$ standard deviation (SD) for AUC, balanced accuracy (BACC), and the feature-averaged Spearman correlation $\bar{\rho}$ between predicted and reference values across the 102 features.
