\subsection{Ablation Study}
Table~\ref{tab:ablation} evaluates the key designs of FARL and FGBM. As shown, omitting family balancing slightly reduces mean AUC on both datasets, whereas removing joint optimization causes larger declines of 2.57 and 6.25 percentage points on ThyroidXL and TN3K, respectively. In contrast to the consistent AUC reductions, measurement fidelity exhibits different trends across datasets. With separate training, $\bar{\rho}$ decreases slightly from 91.34 to 91.22 on ThyroidXL but increases from 84.19 to 84.90 on TN3K. This difference underscores the importance of considering measurement fidelity alongside classification performance. Moreover, without the fidelity loss, $\bar{\rho}$ decreases on both datasets, accompanied by lower AUC. These results show that fidelity guidance helps preserve the correspondence between predicted and measured radiomic features, supporting the interpretation of feature contributions.
