\begin{abstract}
\vspace{-1mm}
This paper proposes a \emph{progressive Bayesian} multiple-input multiple-output (MIMO) sensing framework for multiple targets over multiple stages. We consider a practical yet challenging scenario where the targets' angles are unknown and random parameters to be estimated, while the targets' reflection coefficients are unknown \emph{nuisance} parameters. With an initial prior probability density function (PDF) for the targets' angles, we progressively update the prior PDF for each sensing stage as the \emph{posterior PDF} obtained from the previous stage, based on which Bayesian transmit beamforming optimization is performed to minimize the sum posterior Cram\'er-Rao bound (PCRB) in estimating the targets' angles and Bayesian sensing is performed with the help of new observations in this stage. To analytically characterize the intractable and high-dimensional posterior PDF with low complexity, we propose a \emph{variational Bayesian inference} based approach which derives a surrogate posterior PDF in \emph{closed form} with only \emph{polynomial complexity} over the number of targets, in sharp contrast to existing numerical calculation approaches with \emph{exponential complexity}. Numerical results validate the efficacy of our proposed framework in progressively refining sensing performance.\looseness=-1
\end{abstract}
\vspace{-1mm}
\begin{keywords}
Multiple-input multiple-output (MIMO) radar, progressive sensing, conditional posterior Cram\'er-Rao bound (PCRB), variational Bayesian inference.
\end{keywords}
\vspace{-5mm}
\section{Introduction}
\vspace{-3mm}
Multiple-input multiple-output (MIMO) radar is a promising technology due to its capability of flexible transmit beamforming and high-resolution target sensing \cite{li2008mimo}. Existing studies on the transmit beamforming optimization for MIMO radar generally considered the case where ``predictions'' of the unknown parameters are available, based on which the Cram\'er-Rao bound (CRB) was characterized as a function of such predictions and adopted as the optimization performance metric \cite{li2008mimo}. However, such predictions may be inaccurate or unavailable. Recently, there has been a series of works on Bayesian beamforming which leveraged the complete statistical prior distribution information of the unknown parameters. The \emph{posterior Cram\'er-Rao bound (PCRB)} was adopted as the beamforming optimization performance metric which is a lower bound of the mean-squared error (MSE) with any estimator independent of the unknown parameters' true values \cite{ghaddar2026active,liu2026precoding,ghaddar2025active,hou2026sequential,xu2023mimo1,yao2024optimal,wang2025hybrid,zheng2025beyond}. When such prior distribution information is available, Bayesian sensing can also be performed 
\cite{tipping2001sparse,donoho2009message,tzikas2008variational} via minimum MSE (MMSE), maximum \emph{a posteriori (MAP)}, etc. 

Existing studies typically focused on one sensing stage with fixed prior. If multiple sensing stages are available, \emph{progressive sensing}, also called active sensing \cite{sohrabi2021deep,sohrabi2022active}, can apply different transmit beamforming and sensing strategies over multiple stages based on progressively updated prior information and observations, where the posterior probability density function (PDF) obtained in the previous stage can serve as the prior PDF for the current stage. However, a systematic study on such a progressive Bayesian multi-target sensing framework is currently still lacking, which motivates our study in this paper. In particular, although progressive sensing provides opportunities for successive performance refinements, it also brings a fundamental new challenge. Note that both Bayesian beamforming and Bayesian sensing are critically dependent on the posterior PDF from the previous stage, which is notoriously difficult to characterize especially for multi-target sensing. This thus motivated numerical calculation approaches by approximating the continuous ranges of the unknown parameters via discrete grids, for which the complexity is \emph{exponential} over the number of targets and prohibitive with a large number of targets. To reduce such complexity, a variational line spectral estimation (VALSE) algorithm \cite{badiu2017variational} was proposed to approximate the complex joint posterior PDF of all unknown parameters with a tractable surrogate PDF. This idea was also extended to multi-dimensional \cite{zhang2020multidimensional} and group-sparse line spectra models \cite{moderl2024variational}. However, existing VALSE-based works focused on single-input multiple-output (SIMO) or illuminators of opportunity systems with single-dimensional transmit signals without beamforming capability, while both targets' angles and reflection coefficients are jointly estimated under specified prior distributions. For general MIMO radar systems with possible nuisance parameters (e.g., reflection coefficients), there still lacks a low-complexity approach for tractable posterior PDF characterization.
