This paper addresses the above challenges by establishing a scalable \emph{progressive Bayesian} multi-target MIMO sensing framework with a \emph{variational Bayesian inference} based posterior PDF characterization approach, as shown in Fig. \ref{Fig_System_Model}. We consider a practical scenario where the targets' angles are the only desired parameters, while the reflection coefficients are nuisance parameters. In each sensing stage, a multi-antenna base station (BS) sends beamformed probing signals, which are reflected by the targets and received back at the BS receiver as observations. Next, a tractable \emph{closed-form} surrogate posterior PDF is obtained via the proposed variational Bayesian inference based approach with only \emph{polynomial complexity} over the number of targets, which is then employed as the prior PDF for Bayesian beamforming optimization and Bayesian sensing in the subsequent stage. Numerical results demonstrate the progressive refinement of sensing performance via our proposed framework, and the superiority of it over various benchmark schemes.
\vspace{-7mm}
\section{System Model}\label{section_sys_model}
\vspace{-4mm}
Consider a mono-static MIMO radar system with a BS equipped with $N_t\geq1$ transmit antennas and $N_r\geq1$ co-located receive antennas under a uniform linear array (ULA) layout. The system aims to progressively sense the \emph{unknown} and \emph{random} (azimuth) angles of $M\geq1$ point targets that remain static over $S\geq 1$ stages based on the received echoes of the signals sent from the BS and reflected by the targets (i.e., \emph{observations}) as well as the prior PDF information (i.e., \emph{prior}). Let $\theta_m\in [-\frac{\pi}{2},\frac{\pi}{2})$ denote the angle of the $m$-th target and $\bm{\theta}=[\theta_1,...,\theta_M]^T$ denote the collection of all $M$ angles to be sensed. To draw fundamental insights, we consider a line-of-sight (LoS) channel between the BS and each target. Let $\alpha_{m}\!\!=\!\!\frac{\lambda}{(4\pi)^{3/2}r_m^2}e^{\!-j\frac{4\pi r_m}{\lambda}}\psi_{m}\!=\!\alpha_{m}^\mathrm{R}\!+\!j\alpha_{m}^\mathrm{I}\!\in\! \mathbb{C}$ denote the overall complex reflection coefficient for the $m$-th target which is unknown and remains unchanged over the $S$ stages, with $\lambda$ being the wavelength in meter (m), $r_m$ being the distance between the BS and the $m$-th target in m, and $\psi_{m}\in\mathbb{C}$ being the radar cross-section (RCS) coefficient. Note that $\alpha_m$'s are \emph{unknown nuisance parameters} whose collection is denoted by $\bm{\alpha}=[\alpha_1,...,\alpha_M]^T$. 
\vspace{-5.5mm}
\subsection{Observation Model}
\vspace{-3mm}
Let $\bm{a}(\theta_m)\!\!\in\!\! \mathbb{C}^{N_t\!\times\! 1}$ and $\bm{b}(\theta_m)\!\!\in\!\! \mathbb{C}^{N_r\!\times\! 1}$ denote the transmit/receive array steering vectors with $a_i(\theta_m)= e^{-\!j\pi ( N_t\!-\!2i+1 )\!\sin\! \theta _m/2},\forall i$ and $b_i(\theta_m)=e^{-\!j\pi ( N_r\!-\!2j+1 )\!\sin\! \theta _m/2},\forall i$. Let $L$ denote the number of samples in each stage, and $\bm{X}_{s}\in\mathbb{C}^{N_t\times L}$ denote the collection of probing signals sent from the transmitter in each $s$-th stage. By further assuming all $M$ targets are within the same range bin \cite{li2007range}, the collection of echo signals received in each $s$-th stage is given by
\vspace{-1mm}
\begin{align}
\bm{Y}_s=\sum_{m=1}^M\alpha_{m}\bm{b}(\theta_m) \bm{a}^H(\theta_m)\bm{X}_s+\bm{N}_s,\quad \forall s\in\mathcal{S},	\label{Y}
\end{align}
where $\bm{N}_s\in \mathbb{C}^{N_r\times L}$ with $[\bm{N}_s]_{i,j}\sim\mathcal{CN}(0,\sigma^2),\forall i,j$ denotes the circularly symmetric complex Gaussian (CSCG) noise at the BS receiver, with $\sigma^2$ denoting the average receiver noise power at each antenna, and $\mathcal{S}=\{1,...,S\}$. Let $\bm{R}_{X,s}=\frac{1}{L}\bm{X}_{s}\bm{X}_{s}^H$ denote the transmit sample covariance matrix at the $s$-th stage, which is under a per-stage transmit power constraint $\mathrm{tr}(\bm{R}_{X,s})\leq P,\ \forall s\in\mathcal{S}$.  
\vspace{-5.5mm}
\subsection{Prior Model}
\vspace{-3mm}
We consider independent targets where all the unknown parameters in $\bm{\theta}$ and $\bm{\alpha}$ are independent of each other. Let $p_{\alpha_m}(\alpha_{m})$ denote the prior PDF of $\alpha_m$. The prior PDF of the nuisance parameters in $\bm{\alpha}$ is thus given by $p_{\bm{\alpha}}\!(\bm{\alpha})\!\!=\!\!\prod_{m=1}^Mp_{\alpha_m}\!(\alpha_{m})$, which is known from targets' properties and used throughout the $S$ stages \cite{liu2026precoding}. On the other hand, the initial prior PDF of $\bm{\theta}$ before the first stage is denoted by $p_{\bm{\Theta},1}\!(\bm{\theta})$, which is assumed to be known from historic data or target appearance pattern \cite{yao2024optimal,ghaddar2026active,liu2026precoding,hou2026sequential,ghaddar2025active,xu2023mimo1,wang2025hybrid,zheng2025beyond}. Let $\bm{Y}^{(s)}\overset{\Delta}{=}\{\bm{Y}_1,...,\bm{Y}_s\}$ denote the collection of observed echo signals after the $s$-th stage. The posterior PDF of $\bm{\theta}$ at the end of each $s$-th stage is given by 
 \begin{align}\label{posterior}
p_s(\bm{\theta}|\bm{Y}^{(s)})=\frac{p(\bm{Y}^{(s)}|\bm{\theta})}{p(\bm{Y}^{(s)})}p_{\bm{\Theta},1}(\bm{\theta}),\quad \forall s\in \mathcal{S},
\end{align}
with $p(\bm{Y}^{(s)}|\bm{\theta})$ denoting the conditional PDF of $\bm{Y}^{(s)}$ given $\bm{\theta}$ and $p(\bm{Y}^{(s)})$ the PDF of $\bm{Y}^{(s)}$ which is a constant (i.e., model evidence \cite{bishop2006pattern}). Then, the prior PDF of $\bm{\theta}$ for the $(s+1)$-th stage is updated as 
\begin{align}\label{prior}
p_{\bm{\Theta},s+1}(\bm{\theta})=p_s(\bm{\theta}|\bm{Y}^{(s)}),\quad \forall s\in \mathcal{S}\backslash\{S\}.
\end{align}
Based on the updated prior PDF, transmit beamforming optimization and receiver sensing are performed. By progressively updating the prior PDF before each stage with new observations and designing the transmit beamforming accordingly, the multi-target sensing performance can be progressively refined over stages, as detailed below.
\vspace{-5mm}
\section{Proposed Progressive Bayesian Multi-Target MIMO Sensing Framework}
\vspace{-4mm}
In this section, we present our proposed progressive Bayesian multi-target MIMO sensing framework, which is illustrated in Fig. \ref{Fig_System_Model}. 
\vspace{-4mm}
\subsection{Bayesian Beamforming at the Transmitter}\label{section_PCRB_optimization} 
\vspace{-3mm}
At the start of each $s$-th stage, the BS possesses observations from the previous $s-1$ stages denoted by $\bm{Y}^{(s-1)}$ and the prior PDF of $\bm{\theta}$ denoted by $p_{\bm{\Theta},s}(\bm{\theta})=p_{s-1}(\bm{\theta}|\bm{Y}^{(s-1)})$ given by (\ref{posterior}). The BS aims to design the transmit beamforming characterized by the sample covariance matrix $\bm{R}_{X,s}$ such that the observations created in the $s$-th stage are best for sensing $\bm{\theta}$. As the MSE is difficult to be characterized explicitly, we adopt the \emph{conditional PCRB} as the beamforming optimization performance metric, which is a lower bound of the MSE with any Bayesian estimator and observation history \cite{zuo2011conditional}.
Denote $\bm{\eta}\!\!=\!\![\theta_{1}\!,...,\!\theta_{M}\!,\alpha_{1}^\mathrm{R}\!,\alpha_{1}^\mathrm{I}\!,...,\!\alpha_{M}^\mathrm{R}\!,\alpha_{M}^\mathrm{I}\!]^T$ as the real-equivalent collection of all unknown parameters. The prior PDF of $\bm{\eta}$ before the $s$-th stage is thus given by $p_{\mathrm{H},s}(\bm{\eta})\!\!=\!\!\prod_{m=1}^M{\!p_{\Theta_m,s}(\theta_m)p_{\alpha_m}(\alpha_{m})}$, where $p_{\Theta_m,s}(\theta_m)=\int p_{\bm{\Theta},s}(\bm{\theta})d\bm{\theta}_{\sim m}$ is the marginal prior PDF of $\theta_m$ and $\bm{\theta}_{\sim m}$ denotes all parameters in $\bm{\theta}$ except for $\theta_m$. The conditional PCRB for estimating $\theta_m$ at the $s$-th stage is given by \cite{zuo2011conditional}
\begin{align}
&\mathrm{PCRB}_{\theta_m,s}^{\mathrm{C}}\label{PCRB_theta_m}
&\!=\!\mathbb{E}_{\bm{Y}_s,\bm{\eta}}\!\left[\frac{\partial\ln p(\bm{\eta},\bm{Y}_s|\bm{Y}^{(s-1)})}{\partial\bm{\eta}}\!\left(\frac{\partial\ln p(\bm{\eta},\bm{Y}_s|\bm{Y}^{(s-1)})}{\partial\bm{\eta}}\right)^H\right]_{m,m}^{-1}. \nonumber
\end{align}
We further consider a mild and practical condition of $\mathbb{E}[\bm{\alpha}]\!=\!0$, which holds under the Swerling -0/-I/-II models for $\psi_m$ when $\psi_m$ and $r_m$ are uncorrelated \cite{swerling1960probability}. Define $\bm{M}(\theta_m)\overset{\Delta}{=}\bm{b}( \theta _m) \bm{a}^H(\theta_m)$ and let $\dot{\bm{M}}(\theta_m)$ denote its derivative. We further denote $\bm{A}_{s,m}=\int{\!\dot{\bm{M}}^H\!(\theta_m)\dot{\bm{M}}(\theta_m)p_{\bm{\Theta},s}(\bm{\theta})d\bm{\theta}}\!\succeq\! \bm{0}$, $\beta_m\!=\!\frac{2L\mathbb{E}_{\alpha_m}[|\alpha_m|^2]}{\sigma^2}$, and $\bm{F}_{\mathrm{P},s}=\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\eta}}\Big[\frac{\partial \ln ( p_{\mathrm{H},s}(\bm{\eta}))}{\partial \bm{\eta}}\big( \frac{\partial \ln (p_{\mathrm{H},s}(\bm{\eta}))}{\partial \bm{\eta}}\big)^H\Big]$. The conditional PCRB for the MSE in sensing $\theta_m$ can be then expressed as $\mathrm{PCRB}_{\theta_m,s}^{\mathrm{C}}=\frac{1}{\beta_m\mathrm{tr}(\bm{A}_{s,m}\bm{R}_{X,s})+[ \bm{F}_{\mathrm{P},s}]_{m,m}}$. Based on this, we formulate the following Bayesian transmit beamforming optimization problem to minimize the sum PCRB among all targets:
\vspace{-1mm}\begin{equation}
 \mbox{(P1)}  \min_{\bm{R}_{X,s}\succeq \mathbf{0}:\, \mathrm{tr}(\bm{R}_{X,s}) \leq P}\ \sum_{m=1}^M\!\frac{1}{\beta_m\mathrm{tr}(\!\bm{A}_{s,m}\bm{R}_{X,s}\!)\!\!+\!\![ \bm{F}_{\mathrm{P},s}]_{m,m}}.\label{P1obj}
\vspace{-1mm}\end{equation}
Note that (P1) is a convex problem and can be solved via the interior-point method \cite{boyd2004convex}. With the progressive prior PDF update, the transmit beamforming will progressively concentrate more power around the actual values of $\bm{\theta}$'s, thereby enabling better sensing accuracy.
\vspace{-5mm}
\subsection{Bayesian Sensing at the Receiver}
\vspace{-3mm}
At the end of each $s$-th stage, the BS possesses observations from the previous $s$ stages denoted by $\bm{Y}^{(s)}$ and the prior PDF of $\bm{\theta}$ denoted by $p_{\bm{\Theta},s}(\bm{\theta})$. The posterior PDF $p_s(\bm{\theta}|\bm{Y}^{(s)})$ can be then calculated from (\ref{posterior}), based on which the MMSE estimator can be obtained as $\hat{\bm{\theta}}_\mathrm{MMSE}=\int\bm{\theta}p_s(\bm{\theta}|\bm{Y}^{(s)})d\bm{\theta}$ and the marginal MAP estimator can be obtained as $\hat{\bm{\theta}}_\mathrm{mMAP}=\mathrm{arg}{\max}_{\bm{\theta}}\ p_s(\bm{\theta}|\bm{Y}^{(s)})$ \cite{doucet2002marginal,liu2013variational}.\looseness=-1 
\vspace{-5mm}
\subsection{Complexity with Numerical Posterior PDF Calculation}
\vspace{-2mm}
Note that the posterior PDF $p_{s-1}(\bm{\theta}|\bm{Y}^{(s-1)})$ or $p_{s}(\bm{\theta}|\bm{Y}^{(s)})$
needs to be known for both the calculation of $\bm{A}_{s,m}$'s and $[\bm{F}_{\mathrm{P},s}]_{m,m}$'s in Bayesian beamforming and the calculation of $\hat{\bm{\theta}}_\mathrm{MMSE}$ or $\hat{\bm{\theta}}_\mathrm{mMAP}$ in Bayesian sensing. However, the posterior PDF is a complex function of the previous observations and the prior PDF, which is intractable to be characterized analytically and explicitly. Hence, numerical calculation is generally needed by approximating the continuous range of $\theta_m$ with $\xi_\theta$ discrete grids and the continuous ranges of $\alpha_m^\mathrm{R}$ and $\alpha_m^\mathrm{I}$ with $\xi_\alpha$ discrete grids. Using this numerical posterior calculation approach, the complexities for Bayesian beamforming and Bayesian sensing are summarized in Table \ref{Table_complexity}, where all complexities increase \emph{exponentially} with the number of targets, $M$, which is prohibitive and unaffordable with a large number of targets. To cross this hurdle, we propose a novel \emph{variational Bayesian inference} based posterior characterization approach which yields an explicit analytical expression of the posterior PDF with \emph{polynomial complexity}.
