\section{Introduction}
Future wireless communication systems aim to support extremely high data rates through large antenna arrays and increased bandwidth \cite{10908559}. Large antenna arrays provide high beamforming gain, improved spatial resolution, and highly directional multi-user transmission \cite{8732419,10365224}. However, these benefits come with challenges due to the need for highly directional transmission using limited feedback-rates, sometimes in severe propagation conditions. Moreover, the small wavelengths in future communication systems allow for more antennas in a given aperture \cite{10558818}.
As the physical aperture increases, the propagation behavior changes. In particular, the Fraunhofer distance scales quadratically with the array aperture, enlarging the near-field region \cite{7942128}. Consequently, future wireless systems with very large arrays are expected to serve many users in the near-field region \cite{10496996}. In this region, the planar-wave model is no longer accurate. Instead, the channel must be modeled using a spherical wavefront that depends on both the user's angle and distance \cite{9903389,10098681}. Therefore, beamforming and codebook design must account for both angular and distance variations.
This near-field behavior changes the structure of beamforming codebooks. In conventional far-field systems, codebooks usually cover only the angular domain. For example, the discrete Fourier transform (DFT) codebook in a uniform linear array (ULA) provides far-field beams that uniformly sample the angular domain \cite{6600706}. Codebook-based beamforming is also used in practical standards. In 5G NR, CSI feedback relies on standardized Type-I and Type-II codebooks, where Type-I codebooks provide lower-overhead beam selection and Type-II codebooks provide higher-resolution channel representation at the cost of increased feedback overhead \cite{11346973}. Similarly, millimeter-wave (mmWave) Wi-Fi systems such as IEEE 802.15.3c implement a codebook-based beamforming protocol \cite{5936164}. However, in the near-field environment, the channel contains an additional distance-dependent phase term, causing phase mismatch when far-field codebooks are employed. This has motivated recent work on polar-domain and hierarchical near-field codebooks that jointly search over angle and distance \cite{9693928,6600706,11183591,10195974,10506674,10365224,10304223}.
Despite this progress, the performance of random codebooks in the near-field regime remains completely unexplored. In previous codebook designs, random codebooks have been used to study codebook performance across different quantization rates because they are simple to construct, analytically tractable, and useful as benchmarks for structured codebooks \cite{4100151,7842197,7279196}. However, near-field channels contain distance-dependent phase curvature, which changes how beams concentrate energy. Therefore, the impact of near-field propagation on the performance of random codebooks remains largely unknown.
In this work, we investigate the performance of random codebooks for near-field beamforming. We evaluate four classes of random codebooks: random vector quantization (RVQ), random phase (RP), random far-field (RFF), and random near-field (RNF) codebooks. RVQ draws beamforming vectors independently from the uniform distribution on the complex unit sphere and is widely used as a benchmark in limited-feedback beamforming \cite{4100151}. RP uses constant-modulus beamforming vectors with random antenna phases and has been studied for mmWave initial access and channel-parameter estimation \cite{7842197}. RFF consists of randomly selected far-field steering vectors and is motivated by opportunistic random beamforming in mmWave systems \cite{7279196}. Unlike RVQ, RP, and RFF, there is no prior work on RNF codebooks. We introduce RNF as a near-field random benchmark, where each codeword is generated by randomly sampling an angle-distance pair and forming the corresponding near-field steering vector. These codebooks represent different levels of channel-structure information: RVQ randomizes both antenna amplitudes and phases; RP maintains constant antenna amplitudes while randomizing the phases; RFF imposes a linear phase structure of far-field steering vectors; and RNF samples directly from the near-field steering vector set.
The main contributions of this work are summarized as follows:
\begin{itemize}
 \item We characterize the beamforming-gain distributions of RVQ, RP, RFF, and RNF codebooks in near-field channels and compare their limiting behavior as the codebook size increases.
 For the RP codebook, we derive two approximations for the beamforming-gain distribution. The first is a large-array bulk approximation based on the central limit theorem, while the second describes the probability of obtaining a beamforming gain close to one.
 \item For the RFF codebook, we derive an analytical approximation for the maximum gain achievable by far-field beams when serving a near-field user, along with the corresponding maximizing far-field beam direction. We also show that, even as the codebook size increases, RFF is limited by the residual phase mismatch between far-field beams and the near-field channel.
 \item For the RNF codebook, we characterize the probability of obtaining a beamforming gain close to one. We show that this probability decreases only linearly as the target gain approaches one.
 \item We compare the asymptotic maximum-gain scaling of the codebooks and show that RNF has the most favorable scaling, followed by RP and RVQ, while RFF is limited due to phase mismatch.
\end{itemize}
