\subsubsection{Asymptotic CDF of \texorpdfstring{$\max_{k} \nu_{k}$}{max k nu k}}
We now characterize the distribution of the best gain among $K$ independently generated RFF codewords. For a user located at $(\theta_t,r_t)$, let
\begin{align}
 F_{\nu\mid \theta_t,r_t}(\gamma)
 \triangleq
 \Pr\{\nu \le \gamma \mid \theta_t,r_t\}
\end{align}
denote the conditional CDF of the gain achieved by a single randomly generated far-field beam. Since the $K$ codewords are generated independently, the corresponding gains $\{\nu_k\}_{k =1}^{K}$ are conditionally i.i.d., and therefore $\Pr \{ \max_{ k} \nu_k \le \gamma \mid \theta_t,r_t \} = (F_{\nu\mid\theta_t,r_t}(\gamma))^K$. Averaging over the user-location distribution yields
\begin{align}\label{cdf_ff_1}
 \Pr\{\max_{ 1\leq k \leq K} &\nu_k \le \gamma \} = \notag 
 &\iint
 \big(F_{\nu\mid\theta_t,r_t}(\gamma)\big)^K
 f_\Theta(\theta_t)f_{R_t}(r_t)\, d\theta_t\,dr_t.
\end{align}
To evaluate \eqref{cdf_ff_1}, it is useful to distinguish between user locations for which the threshold $\gamma$ is unachievable by any far-field beam and those for which it is achievable. Define 
\begin{align}
 \mathcal R(\gamma)
 \triangleq
 \{(\theta_t,r_t): \nu_{\max}(\theta_t,r_t)\le \gamma\}.
\end{align}
The set $\mathcal{R}(\gamma)$ contains the user locations for which even the best far-field beam achieves a gain of at most $\gamma$. For such locations, $F_{\nu\mid\theta_t,r_t}(\gamma)=1$. Splitting the integral in \eqref{cdf_ff_1} over $\mathcal{R}(\gamma)$ and its complement $\mathcal{R}^{c}(\gamma)$ gives
\begin{align}\label{cdf_ff_2}
 \Pr&\{\max_{1 \leq k \leq K} \nu_k \le \gamma\}
 =
 \iint_{\mathcal R(\gamma)}
 f_\Theta(\theta_t)f_{R_t}(r_t)\,d\theta_t\,dr_t \notag
 &\quad+
 \iint_{\mathcal R^c(\gamma)}
 \big(F_{\nu\mid\theta_t,r_t}(\gamma)\big)^K
 f_\Theta(\theta_t)f_{R_t}(r_t)\,d\theta_t\,dr_t.
\end{align}
Denote the first and second integrals in \eqref{cdf_ff_2} by $I_1(\gamma)$ and $I_2(K,\gamma)$, respectively.
For every \((\theta_t,r_t)\in \mathcal R^c(\gamma)\), we have $\nu_{\max}(\theta_t,r_t) > \gamma$. Hence, there exists a non-zero number of far-field directions for which the gain exceeds $\gamma$, and therefore $F_{\nu\mid\theta_t,r_t}(\gamma) < 1$. It follows that $\lim_{K \to \infty} I_2(K,\gamma) = 0$. Thus, from \eqref{cdf_ff_2},
\begin{align}
 \lim_{K\to\infty}\Pr\{\max_{1 \leq k \leq K} \nu_k \le \gamma\}
 =
 I_1(\gamma),
\end{align}
where $I_1(\gamma) = \Pr\left\{ \nu_{\max}(\theta_t,r_t)\le \gamma \right\}$. Therefore, unlike the RVQ and RP codebooks, the RFF codebook does not satisfy
\begin{align}
 \lim_{K\to\infty}\Pr\{\max_{1 \leq k \leq K} \nu_k \le \gamma\}=0
\end{align}
for every $\gamma \in [0,1)$. This occurs because there are user locations for which the threshold $\gamma$ cannot be exceeded by any far-field beam, regardless of the codebook size. This reflects the fundamental residual mismatch between far-field codebooks and near-field channels.
\subsection{Random Near-Field (RNF) Codebook}\label{RNF_section}
We next consider the RNF codebook. Unlike RFF, whose codewords are restricted to far-field steering vectors, RNF codewords are generated directly from the near-field steering vector set. Thus, each RNF codeword contains both the linear and quadratic phase components of the near-field channel, making RNF a natural benchmark for near-field beamforming.
Each RNF codeword $\mathbf{w}_{k} \in \mathcal{F}$ is generated by first sampling a random direction $\theta_k \sim f_{\Theta}(\theta)$ and a random distance $r_k \sim f_{R}(r)$, and then forming the corresponding near-field steering vector. The $k$-th codeword is then given by
\begin{align}
 \mathbf{w}_k = \mathbf{a}(\theta_k,r_k).
\end{align}
For a user with channel vector $\mathbf{h}(\theta_t,r_t) = \alpha \mathbf{a}(\theta_t,r_t)$, the beamforming gain of the $k$-th RNF codeword is
\begin{align}
 \nu_k
 = \left\lvert \mathbf{a}^{\mathsf H}(\theta_t,r_t)\mathbf{a}(\theta_k,r_k) \right\rvert^2.
\end{align}
This gain is large when the sampled pair $(\theta_k,r_k)$ lies close to the true user location $(\theta_t,r_t)$. Therefore, the high-gain behavior of the RNF codebook is governed by the local geometry of the near-field steering vector in the joint angular-distance domain.
The following lemma characterizes the high-gain behavior of a single RNF codeword.
\begin{lemma}\label{lemma_nf}
 For $\gamma = 1 - \epsilon$ with $\epsilon \ll 1$, the CDF of the RNF beamforming gain satisfies
 \begin{align}
 F_{\nu}(\gamma) = 1 - G_{M}(1-\gamma) + \mathcal{O}((1 - \gamma)^{3/2}),
 \end{align}
 where $G_{M}$ is a constant determined by the local near-field steering vector geometry and the sampling distribution.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
 See Appendix \ref{appendix_nf}.
\end{proof}
Lemma \ref{lemma_nf} shows that as $\gamma$ approaches one, the right-tail probability of the RNF gain scales linearly with $1-\gamma$. This linear scaling follows from the fact that RNF codewords are sampled from the same two-parameter near-field model as the channel. Therefore, a high-gain event occurs when the randomly chosen pair $(\theta_k,r_k)$ falls within a sufficiently small neighborhood of $(\theta_t,r_t)$.
Using the order-statistics result from \eqref{order_statistics}, the maximum RNF gain over $K$ independently generated codewords satisfies $\Pr\left\{\max_{1 \leq k \leq K} \nu_k \le \gamma\right\} = \big(F_{\nu}(\gamma)\big)^K$. Since $F_{\nu}(\gamma) < 1$ for every fixed $\gamma < 1$, it follows that 
\begin{align}
 \max_{1\leq k\leq K}\nu_k
 \xrightarrow[K\to\infty]{\mathsf p}
 1.
\end{align}
\section{Simulations}\label{results}
\subsection{Limiting Behavior of the Maximum Beamforming Gain}
We now summarize the limiting behavior of the maximum beamforming gain for the four random codebooks. For RVQ, RP, and RNF, the single-codeword CDF satisfies $F_{\nu}(\gamma) < 1$ for every fixed $\gamma < 1$. Therefore, using the order-statistics result in \eqref{order_statistics}, the maximum gain converges in probability to one as the $K \to \infty$.
Using the general maximum gain characterization in Section \ref{section_codebook_design}, we now compare the high-gain tails of the different codebooks. For RVQ, RP, and RNF, the single codeword high-gain probabilities satisfy $p_{M}^{\mathrm{RVQ}}(\epsilon) = \epsilon^{M-1}$, $p_{M}^{\mathrm{RP}}(\epsilon) \sim C_M \epsilon^{\frac{M-1}{2}}$, and $p_{M}^{\mathrm{RNF}}(\epsilon) \sim G_M \epsilon$, respectively. Since the maximum gain exceeds $1- \epsilon$ with high probability when $K p_M(\epsilon) \to \infty$, the codebook sizes required to achieve $\Pr \{ \max_{k}\ \nu_k \geq 1 - \epsilon \} \to 1$ scale as
\begin{align}
 K_{\mathrm{RVQ}} &\gg \epsilon^{-(M-1)}, 
 K_{\mathrm{RP}} &\gg \frac{1}{C_M}\epsilon^{-\frac{M-1}{2}}, 
 K_{\mathrm{RNF}} &\gg \frac{1}{G_M} \epsilon^{-1}.
\end{align}
Thus, RVQ requires exponential growth in $M$ to maintain a fixed target gap $\epsilon$ from unit gain. RP also requires exponential growth in $M$, but with a smaller exponent because the constant-amplitude removes amplitude fluctuations. RNF has the most favorable high-gain scaling since its right-tail probability is linear in $\epsilon$.
In contrast, the RFF codebook is restricted to far-field steering vectors. Increasing the RFF codebook size allows the gain to approach the maximum achievable beamforming gain $\nu_{\max}(\theta_t,r_t)$, but this value is below one for finite-distance near-field users. Therefore, RVQ, RP, and RNF can asymptotically achieve unit gain as $K \to \infty$, whereas RFF saturates below unit gain. 
\subsection{Performance of Different Codebooks}
