\end{figure}
In this section, we present the simulation results. The operating frequency is 10 GHz. The user lies in a region with $\theta_{t} \in [-1,1]$ and $r_t \in [r_{\min} ,100\ \text{m}]$.
Fig. \ref{fig2} compares the performance of the RVQ and RP codebooks. As the number of antennas increases, the performance of both codebooks degrades significantly. This is because the probability that the phase contributions from all antennas add constructively decreases with $M$, resulting in a lower beamforming gain. Moreover, the RP codebook consistently outperforms the RVQ codebook because the RP codebook preserves the constant-modulus structure across the antenna array and randomizes only the phases. In contrast, RVQ introduces randomness in both amplitudes and phases, which can reduce the effective contribution of some antennas even when the phases are perfectly aligned. This behavior is consistent with the high-gain tail analysis, the RP tail decays as $(1-\gamma)^{\frac{M-1}{2}}$, whereas the RVQ tail decays faster as $(1-\gamma)^{M-1}$. 
Fig. \ref{fig_rff} shows the performance of the RFF codebook. As the codebook size $K$ increases, the maximum beamforming gain improves, which is expected because a larger codebook provides more far-field directions and therefore increases the probability of selecting a beam that is well aligned with the user. However, as the number of antennas $M$ increases, the performance degrades. This is because larger arrays produce narrower far-field beams, so even a small mismatch between the user direction and the selected beam direction can result in lower beamforming gain. In addition, the near-field quadratic phase variation is more significant for larger arrays.
Furthermore, as shown in Section \ref{RFF}, the RFF codebook does not generally achieve unit gain even as $K$ becomes large. Increasing $K$ only allows the codebook to approach the best far-field beam for a given near-field user. Figure \ref{fig_rff} supports this result, since the maximum gain does not equal one even when the number of codewords is large.
Fig. \ref{fig3} shows the performance of the RNF codebook for $M = 128$. As the codebook size $K$ increases, the CDF shifts toward the right, which means that the maximum beamforming gain becomes larger with a higher probability. This is expected because a larger RNF codebook contains more randomly sampled near-field steering vectors, increasing the probability that one of the sampled pairs $(\theta_k,r_k)$ lies close to the true user location $(\theta_t,r_t)$. Moreover, unlike the RFF codebook, as $K$ increases, the maximum beamforming gain converges in probability to one, consistent with the limiting behavior derived in Section \ref{RNF_section}. This is evident from the figure, as $K$ increases, the CDF remains close to zero over most of the gain range and rises sharply near $\gamma = 1$, indicating that the maximum gain is concentrated near one.
Fig. \ref{fig4} compares the CDF of the maximum beamforming gain for the RVQ, RP, RFF, and RNF codebooks. It can be observed that RNF achieves the best performance, followed by RFF, RP, and RVQ. Here, better performance means that the CDF is shifted farther to the right, or equivalently, that the probability $\Pr\{ \max_{k} \nu_k \leq \gamma\}$ is smaller for a given threshold $\gamma$. The RNF codebook provides the best performance, which is intuitive because its codewords capture the near-field phase structure more accurately than those of other codebooks. The RFF codebook performs well for a fixed codebook size because its codewords have a structured phase profile, which is better than RP and RVQ. However, although RFF outperforms RP and RVQ for a fixed $K$, it is asymptotically inferior to both, whereas RNF provides the best performance across all values of $K$.
\section{Conclusions}\label{conclusions}
In this work, we studied four classes of randomly generated codebooks for near-field communications. 
We characterized their beamforming gain distributions and compared the limiting behavior of the maximum gain as the codebook size increases. 
For the RP codebook, we derived a large-array bulk approximation and a right-tail approximation near unit gain. 
For the RFF codebook, we derived an analytical expression for the maximum gain achievable by far-field beams and characterized the corresponding maximizing far-field direction. For the RNF codebook, we characterized the right-tail behavior near unit gain and showed that the probability scales linearly with the gap from unit gain. 
The results show that the performance of RVQ and RP codebooks deteriorates significantly as the array size grows, while RNF provides the most favorable high-gain scaling. RFF can outperfrom RVQ and RP for finite codebook sizes, but its maximum gain saturates below one for finite-distance near-field users. 
Future work includes studying whether far-field beams can be used for low-overhead near-field beam training and whether their measurements contain sufficient information to estimate both the user's angle and distance. Another direction is to characterize the geometry of the near-field high-gain region, which can guide the design of more efficient near-field codebooks.
