Define $u(p) \triangleq \frac{p}{\sin(\pi Ap)}$. Then, from \eqref{define_f}, $f_p(\theta_t, r_t) = u(p)\sin(\pi(M-p)Ap)$. We first approximate \(u(p)\). From \eqref{define_A_range} $A(\theta_t,r_t) \in \left[0, A_{\max} \right]$. This bound shows that the maximum possible value of $A(\theta_t,r_t)$ decreases as $M$ increases. Therefore, over the entire considered user region, $A(\theta_t,r_t)$ is small for large $M$, which justifies the use of small-angle expansion.
Using $\sin x = x + \mathcal{O}(x^3)$, with \(x=\pi Ap\), we obtain $\sin(\pi Ap) = \pi Ap\left(1+\mathcal{O}\left(A^2p^2\right)\right)$. Substituting this into \(u(p)\) gives $u(p) = \frac{1}{\pi A \left(1+\mathcal{O}\left(A^2p^2\right)\right)}$. Since $p\in [1,M-1]$ and $A \in \left[0, A_{\max} \right]$, we have $A^2p^2 \leq A_{\max}^2 (M-1)^2 = \mathcal{O}(\frac{1}{M})$. Thus, for all \(p\in\{1,2,\ldots,M-1\}\),
\begin{align}
 u(p)
 = \frac{1}{\pi A}\left(1+\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{1}{M}\right)\right).
\end{align}
Hence, for large \(M\), \(u(p)\) is approximately constant over the entire range of interest. Next, notice that the second factor in $f_p(\theta_t, r_t)$ is exactly symmetric under the reflection \(p \mapsto M-p\).
