From Lemma \ref{lemma_1}, the function $f_p(\theta_t, r_t)$ is approximately even about $p= \frac{M}{2}$ for large $M$. Therefore, in \eqref{define_derivative},
the summation approximately cancels if $g_p(\theta_k, \theta_t, r_t)$ is odd about \(p=M/2\). Define $C \triangleq A(M-1)+\theta_k-\theta_t$ so that $ g_p(\theta_k, \theta_t, r_t) =\sin(\pi p C)$. For $ g_p(\theta_k, \theta_t, r_t) $ to be odd about $p=M/2$, we require $g_{M-p}(\theta_k, \theta_t, r_t)=-g_p(\theta_k, \theta_t, r_t)$. If $C=\frac{2b}{M}, b\in\mathbb Z$,
then $\pi MC=2b\pi$, and hence $g_{M-p}(\theta_k, \theta_t, r_t) = \sin\left(2b\pi-\pi pC\right) = -g_p(\theta_k, \theta_t, r_t)$. Thus, $g_p(\theta_k, \theta_t, r_t)$ is odd about $p=M/2$ for $\theta_k^{(b)} = \theta_t-A(M-1)+\frac{2b}{M}, b\in\mathbb Z$.

To quantify the residual, write
$
h(p)\triangleq \sin(\pi A p(M-p)).
$
Since
$
\frac{p}{\sin(\pi Ap)} = \frac{1}{\pi A} + \mathcal O(Ap^2)$, we have $ f_p(\theta_t,r_t) = \frac{h(p)}{\pi A} + h(p)\mathcal O(Ap^2)$. Since the first term is exactly even, only the residual contributes. Since $|h(p)|\leq1$ and $|g_p(\theta_k,\theta_t,r_t)|\leq1$, from \eqref{define_derivative},
$ \left. \frac{\partial \nu_k}{\partial \theta_k} \right|_{\theta_k=\theta_k^{(b)}} = \mathcal O(AM)$.
Since $A\leq A_{\max}=\mathcal O(M^{-3/2})$, the derivative is $\mathcal O(M^{-1/2})$. Hence, $\theta_k^{(b)}$ is an approximate stationary direction for large $M$.
\section{Proof of Lemma \ref{lemma_3} }\label{appendix_C}
From Lemma \ref{lemma_2}, the approximate stationary far-field directions of the RFF gain are $\theta_k^{(b)} = \theta_t - (M-1)A(\theta_t,r_t) + \frac{2b}{M}, b \in \mbb{N}_{0}$. 
Recall from \eqref{gain_define_critical_points},
\begin{align}
 G_b(A)
 =
 \frac{1}{M}
 +
 \frac{2}{M^2}
 \sum_{p=1}^{M-1}
 \cos\left(\pi p\frac{2b}{M}\right)
 \frac{\sin(\pi(M-p)Ap)}{\sin(\pi Ap)}.
\end{align}
We can rewrite $G_{b}(A)$ by substituting the $b$th candidate into the gain expression in \eqref{Y_k_original} as
\begin{align}
 G_b(A)
 =
 \frac{1}{M^2}
 \left|
 \sum_{n=0}^{M-1}
 e^{j\pi A(n^2-(M-1)n)}
 e^{j2\pi b n/M}
 \right|^2 .
\end{align}
The natural scaling of the curvature parameter is obtained from the quadratic phase term $\pi A n^2$. Since the largest antenna index is of order $M$, the total quadratic phase variation across the array is of order $AM^2$. Therefore, we define the scaled curvature parameter $\tau \triangleq AM^2.$ We now set $A=\tau/M^2$ and $x_n=n/M$. Then $A(n^2-(M-1)n) = \tau(x_n^2-x_n) + \frac{\tau x_n}{M}$. Hence, for fixed $\tau$ and fixed candidate index $q$,
\begin{align}
 e^{j \pi A(n^2 - (M-1)n)} e^{j 2 \pi b n/M} = e^{j \pi (\tau (x_n^2 - x_n) + 2 b x_n)} e^{j \pi \tau x_n/M}.
\end{align}
Since $e^{j \pi \tau x_n/M} = 1 + \mathcal{O}(1/M)$ for all $x_n \in [0,1]$, we get
\begin{align}
 \frac{1}{M}
 \sum_{n=0}^{M-1}
 &e^{j\pi A(n^2-(M-1)n)}
 e^{j2\pi b n/M}
 = \notag 
 &\frac{1}{M}
 \sum_{n=0}^{M-1}
 e^{j\pi(\tau(x_n^2-x_n)+2 b x_n)}
 +
 \mathcal O\left(\frac{1}{M}\right).
\end{align}
The summation on the right-hand side is a Riemann sum. Therefore,
\begin{align}
 \frac{1}{M}
 \sum_{n=0}^{M-1}
 e^{j\pi A(n^2-(M-1)n)}
 e^{j2\pi b n/M}
 \to
 \int_0^1
 e^{j\pi(\tau(x^2-x)+2 b x)}
 dx
\end{align}
as $M\to\infty$. Therefore, $G_b\left(\frac{\tau}{M^2}\right)
 \to
 \mathcal G_b(\tau)$,
where
\begin{align}\label{define_G_q_alpha}
 \mathcal G_b(\tau)
 \triangleq
 \left|
 \int_0^1
 e^{j\pi(\tau(x^2-x)+2bx)}
 dx
 \right|^2.
\end{align}
The gain expression in \eqref{define_G_q_alpha} has a useful interpretation at $\tau = 0$. For $b=0$, $\mathcal{G}_{0}(0) =1$. For $b \geq 1 $, $\mathcal{G}_{q}(0) = 0$. Thus, when the near-field curvature is negligible, the candidate $q = 0$ is optimal. The integral in \eqref{define_G_q_alpha} can be expressed using the Fresnel integrals. Define $C(u)
 =
 \int_0^u
 \cos\left(\frac{\pi t^2}{2}\right)dt$ and $S(u)
 =
 \int_0^u
 \sin\left(\frac{\pi t^2}{2}\right)dt$. Completing the square in \eqref{define_G_q_alpha} gives
\begin{align}\label{fresnel_gain}
 \mathcal G_b(\tau)
 =
 \frac{1}{2\tau}
 \left[
 \left(C(u_b^+)-C(u_b^-)\right)^2
 +
 \left(S(u_b^+)-S(u_b^-)\right)^2
 \right],
\end{align}
where $u_b^-
 =
 -\sqrt{\frac{\tau}{2}}
 +
 \frac{\sqrt{2}b}{\sqrt{\tau}}$ and $u_b^+
 =
 \sqrt{\frac{\tau}{2}}
 +
 \frac{\sqrt{2}b}{\sqrt{\tau}}$.
For each $b \geq 1$, define $\tau_{\mathrm{peak},b}$ as the first local maximum of $\mathcal{G}_{b}(\tau)$. Numerical calculation of \eqref{fresnel_gain} gives
\begin{align}
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
b
&
\tau_{\mathrm{peak},b}
&
\mathcal G_b(\tau_{\mathrm{peak},b})

\hline
1 & 5.28818 & 0.27533 
2 & 8.34186 & 0.17585 
3 & 11.13386 & 0.13140 
4 & 13.79617 & 0.10566 
5 & 16.37723 & 0.08871
\end{array}
\end{align}
Thus, the first local maximum shifts to larger values of $\tau$ as $b$ increases. This shows that higher order candidates become relevant only for larger values of $A(\theta_t,r_t)$. This makes intuitive sense because a larger $A(\theta_t,r_t)$ corresponds to a higher mismatch and a significant beam-split gain pattern.
We now define the transition points. Let $\beta_b$, with $\beta_0 = 0$, denote the scaled transition values corresponding to the sequence $\{ A_{\textrm{th},b} \}$, i.e., $A_{\textrm{th},b} = \frac{\beta_b}{M^2}$. Equivalently, $\beta_b$ is the value of $\tau$ that satisfies $\mathcal{G}_{b-1}(\beta_b) = \mathcal{G}_{b}(\beta_b).$
Numerically evaluating $\{ A_{\textrm{th},b} \}$ from \eqref{define_B}, we get
\begin{align}
\beta_1=4.72317,\quad
\beta_2=7.78690,\quad
\beta_3=10.58573.
\end{align}
We first consider the interval $\beta_1 \leq \tau \leq \beta_2$. The relevant scaled values are
\begin{align}
 \beta_1=4.72317,
 \quad \tau_{\mathrm{peak},1}=5.28818,
 \quad
 \beta_2=7.78690.
\end{align}
The gains using \eqref{fresnel_gain} at these values are
\begin{align}
\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}
b
&
\mathcal G_b(\beta_1)
&
\mathcal G_b(\tau_{\mathrm{peak},1})
&
\mathcal G_b(\beta_2)

\hline
0 & 0.26825 & 0.19072 & 0.08540 
1 & 0.26825 & 0.27533 & 0.17253 
2 & 0.07458 & 0.09642 & 0.17253 
3 & 0.01611 & 0.02308 & 0.07466 
4 & 0.00414 & 0.00598 & 0.02405 
5 & 0.00140 & 0.00197 & 0.00773
\end{array}
\end{align}
At the left endpoint $\beta_1$, the candidates $b=0$ and $b=1$ tie. At the right endpoint $\beta_2$, the candidates $b=1$ and $b=2$ tie. Over this interval, the previous candidate $b=0$ is decreasing, while the higher-order candidates $b \geq 2$ have their first local maxima outside the interval and remain below $b = 1$, with $b = 2$ only tying at $\beta_2$. Therefore, $b = 1$ gives the largest gain on $\beta_1 \leq \tau \leq \beta_2$.
Similarly, consider the interval $\beta_2 \leq \tau \leq \beta_3$. The relevant scaled values are
\begin{align}
 \beta_2=7.78690,
 \quad
 \tau_{\mathrm{peak},2}=8.34186,
 \quad
 \beta_3=10.58573.
\end{align}
The gains using \eqref{fresnel_gain} at these values are
\begin{align}
\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}
b
