At the left endpoint $\beta_2$, the candidates $b=1$ and $b=2$ tie. At the right endpoint $\beta_3$, the candidates $b=2$ and $b=3$ tie. Over this interval, the previous candidate $b=1$ is decreasing, while the higher-order candidates $b \geq 3$ have their first local maxima outside the interval and remain below $b=2$, with $b=3$ only tying at $\beta_3$. Therefore, $b=2$ gives the largest gain on $\beta_2 \leq \tau \leq \beta_3$, except on the sub-interval $(10.50374,10.81127)$ where $\mathcal{G}_0(\tau)$ dominates. This is the exception noted in Remark $\ref{remark_execption}$.
The same pattern is observed for subsequent intervals by numerically evaluating the gains. Hence, for large $M$, if $\beta_{b^{\star}} \leq AM^2 \leq \beta_{b^{\star}+1}$,
or equivalently $A_{\textrm{th},b^{\star}} \leq A \leq A_{\textrm{th},b^{\star}+1}$, then the maximizing far-field direction is $\theta_k^{(b^{\star})}$, up to the exception noted above. Substituting the direction $\theta_k^{(b^{\star})}$ into \eqref{nu_A_B} gives \eqref{max_ff_cb}. This proves the large-$M$ approximation.
\section{Proof of Lemma \ref{lemma_nf}}\label{appendix_nf}
Define the phase difference vector $\boldsymbol{\xi} \triangleq [\xi_0,\ldots,\xi_{M-1}]^{\mathsf T}$, where $\xi_n = \frac{2\pi}{\lambda} \left(r^{(n)}(\theta_k,r_k) - r^{(n)}(\theta_t,r_t) \right)$. Then, the beamforming gain of the $k$-th RNF codeword is $\nu_k = \frac{1}{M^2} \left| \sum_{n=0}^{M-1} e^{-j\xi_n} \right|^2$. Let $\Delta\theta=\theta_k-\theta_t$, $\Delta r=r_k-r_t$, and $ \mbf{o} = \begin{bmatrix} \Delta\theta \, \Delta r \end{bmatrix}^{\mathsf{T}}$. For small offsets around $(\theta_t,r_t)$, a first-order Taylor expansion of the phase map gives $\boldsymbol{\xi} = \mathbf J \mbf{o} + \mathcal O(\| \mbf{o} \|^2)$,
where $\mathbf J\in\mathbb R^{M\times 2}$ is the Jacobian of the phase vector with respect to $(\theta,r)$, evaluated at $(\theta_t,r_t)$.
Using the second-order Taylor expansion $e^{-j\xi_n} = 1-j\xi_n-\frac{\xi_n^2}{2} + \mathcal O(\xi_n^3)$, we obtain
\begin{align}
 \sum_{n=0}^{M-1}e^{-j\xi_n}
 =
 M
 -
 j\sum_{n=0}^{M-1}\xi_n
 -
 \frac{1}{2}\sum_{n=0}^{M-1}\xi_n^2
 +
 \mathcal O(\|\boldsymbol{\xi}\|^3).
\end{align}
Therefore,
\begin{align}
 \left|
 \sum_{n=0}^{M-1}e^{-j\xi_n}
 \right|^2
 =
 M^2
 -
 M\sum_{n=0}^{M-1}\xi_n^2
 +
 \left(\sum_{n=0}^{M-1}\xi_n\right)^2
 +
 \mathcal O(\|\boldsymbol{\xi}\|^3).
\end{align}
Dividing by $M^2$ gives
\begin{align}
 \nu_k
 =
 1
 -
 \frac{1}{M}\sum_{n=0}^{M-1}\xi_n^2
 +
 \frac{1}{M^2}
 \left(\sum_{n=0}^{M-1}\xi_n\right)^2
 +
 \mathcal O(\|\boldsymbol{\xi}\|^3).
\end{align}
Now define $\mathbf P \triangleq \mathbf I_M-\frac{1}{M}\mathbf 1_M\mathbf 1_M^{\mathsf T}$, where $\mathbf I_M$ is the $M \times M$ identity matrix and $\mathbf 1_M$ is the $M \times 1$ all-ones vector. Since
\begin{align}
 \|\mathbf P\boldsymbol{\xi}\|^2
 =
 \sum_{n=0}^{M-1}\xi_n^2
 -
 \frac{1}{M}
 \left(\sum_{n=0}^{M-1}\xi_n\right)^2,
\end{align}
we can write
\begin{align}
 \nu_k
 =
 1
 -
 \frac{1}{M}
 \|\mathbf P\boldsymbol{\xi}\|^2
 +
 \mathcal O(\|\boldsymbol{\xi}\|^3).
\end{align}
Using $\boldsymbol{\xi} = \mathbf J \mbf{o} + \mathcal O(\| \mbf{o} \|^2)$ inside $\lVert \mbf{P} \boldsymbol{\xi} \rVert^2$ gives
\begin{align}
 \|\mathbf P\boldsymbol{\xi}\|^2
 =
 \mbf{o} ^{\mathsf T}
 \mathbf J^{\mathsf T}\mathbf P\mathbf J
 \mbf{o}
 +
 \mathcal O(\|\mbf{o}\|^3).
\end{align}
Thus,
\begin{align}
 \nu_k
 =
 1
 -
 \frac{1}{M}
 \mbf{o}^{\mathsf T}
 \mathbf J^{\mathsf T}\mathbf P\mathbf J
 \mbf{o}
 +
 \mathcal O(\|\mbf{o}\|^3).
\end{align}
Let $\mathbf Q \triangleq \mathbf J^{\mathsf T}\mathbf P\mathbf J$. Thus, the high-gain event
$\nu_k\ge 1-\epsilon$ is, up to higher-order terms, equivalent to $\mbf{o}^{\mathsf T} \mathbf Q \mbf{o} \le M\epsilon$. This inequality defines an ellipse in the $(\Delta\theta,\Delta r)$ plane. Its semi-axis lengths scale as $\sqrt{\epsilon}$, so its area scales linearly with $\epsilon$. The cubic remainder perturbs the boundary by order $\epsilon^{3/2}$, and hence
\begin{align}\label{cdf_ap1}
 \Pr\{\nu_k\ge 1-\epsilon\}
 =
 G_M\epsilon
 +
 \mathcal O(\epsilon^{3/2}),
\end{align}
where $G_M>0$ depends on the local sampling density of $(\theta_k,r_k)$ and on the local geometry through $\mathbf Q$. Using \eqref{cdf_ap1} together with $ 1-F_\nu(1-\epsilon) = \Pr\{\nu\geq 1-\epsilon\}$, and then setting $\epsilon=1-\gamma$ proves the lemma.
