\section*{D. Prompt Templates and Implementation}
\subsection*{Prompt A: Normal vs. Pathological WCE Classification}
The binary prompt assigns each WCE frame to exactly one of two labels, \texttt{N} (normal/non-pathological) or \texttt{P} (pathological). The prompt defines pathological findings to include aphthoid ulceration, ulceration, erythema, edema, stenosis, focal mucosal breaks, depressed lesions, white/yellow ulcer bases, red halos, abnormal redness, swelling, or luminal narrowing. The decision rules instruct the model to choose \texttt{N} only when the mucosa appears continuous and no focal abnormality is visible, and to choose \texttt{P} whenever a definite abnormality is present. In the baseline setting, no refined hypotheses or in-context examples are provided. In the ARISE setting, the refined hypothesis list is inserted under a \emph{Supporting rules} field and the model is asked to return the predicted class together with the ID of the supporting hypothesis when available.
\begin{quote}\small
\textbf{Prompt A template:}
You are a medical assistant specialized in wireless capsule endoscopy image classification.
Classify each WCE image into exactly one binary label: 
\texttt{N} = normal-appearing / non-pathological frame.
\texttt{P} = pathological / abnormal frame.[0.25em]
\textbf{Supporting rules:} [refined hypotheses, ARISE setting only][0.25em]
\textbf{Decision rules:} choose \texttt{N} only for normal, continuous mucosa without focal abnormality; choose \texttt{P} if any visible abnormality is present; do not output a lesion subtype.
\end{quote}
\subsection*{Prompt B: Six-Lesion WCE Classification}
Prompt B is applied only to frames already considered pathological and assigns exactly one lesion label: aphthoid ulceration (\texttt{AU}), erythema (\texttt{E}), edema (\texttt{O}), stenosis (\texttt{S}), ulceration 3--10 mm (\texttt{U3-10}), or ulceration $>$10 mm (\texttt{U>10}). The prompt includes a short description of each class and an ulcer-first decision hierarchy: visible mucosal break, depression, erosion, crater, or white/yellow ulcer base directs the model toward an ulcer subtype; intact-surface redness favors erythema; swollen/enlarged villi with a continuous surface favors edema; and dominant luminal narrowing favors stenosis. The baseline Prompt B uses a fixed set of short class-discrimination rules. In the ARISE setting, these fixed rules are replaced by the refined hypothesis state loaded from disk.
\begin{quote}\small
\textbf{Prompt B template:}
You are a medical assistant specialized in wireless capsule endoscopy image classification.
This image is already considered pathological. Do not choose \texttt{N}.
Classify the pathological subtype into exactly one of: 
\texttt{AU, E, O, S, U3-10, U>10}.[0.25em]
\textbf{Supporting rules:} [fixed short rules for baseline; refined hypotheses for ARISE][0.25em]
\textbf{Decision hierarchy:} ulcerative surface loss $\rightarrow$ ulcer subtype; intact-surface redness $\rightarrow$ \texttt{E}; swollen/enlarged villi with continuous surface $\rightarrow$ \texttt{O}; dominant luminal narrowing $\rightarrow$ \texttt{S}.
\end{quote}
\subsection*{Support and In-Context Examples}
A set of support examples are selected for each task for Stage S2. Seprately, ARISE runs use manually selected labeled image examples as multimodal in-context demonstrations. The demonstrations contain the example image and its class label; the explanatory text stored with each example is used during the final diagnostic evaluation. For Prompt A, GPT and Gemini use four ICL examples, while Qwen2-VL-2B uses two. For Prompt B, the GPT notebook permits up to 12 examples, Gemini uses four, and Qwen2-VL-2B uses two. Qwen uses fewer examples to reduce GPU memory consumption on a Colab T4.
