 \textbf{EchoDino retains fine anatomical boundaries across echocardiographic domains.} 
 Dense segmentation tests whether frozen features support pixel-level delineation of cardiac chambers and the myocardium. We evaluated left ventricular (LV) segmentation on EchoNet-Dynamic and EchoNet-Pediatric, as well as multistructure segmentation on CAMUS, using a one-layer linear segmentation head for all models. EchoDino significantly exceeded DINOv3 for every structural target (Table~\ref{tab:segmentation_unified}; all $p < 0.001$). LV Dice scores reached 0.910 on EchoNet-Dynamic and 0.903 on EchoNet-Pediatric, exceeding PanEcho by 0.023 and 0.043. On the CAMUS dataset, EchoDino achieved Dice coefficients of 0.932 for the LV, 0.869 for the myocardium, and 0.888 for the left atrium—exceeding PanEcho by 0.013, 0.029, and 0.020, respectively. Representative examples (Fig.~\ref{fig:EchoDino_arch}e) show predicted chamber and myocardial regions accurately aligned with reference contours across apical, short-axis, and long-axis views. These consistent gains across three distinct datasets confirm that EchoDino retains highly accurate boundary-level information for multiple cardiac structures across different age groups.
