\subsection{MEMS improves EchoDino's phase-dependent functional predictions}
\textbf{EchoDino and MEMS support ejection-fraction prediction across pediatric and adult cohorts.}
Unlike SHD diagnosis, ejection fraction (EF) prediction relies heavily on temporal dynamics, specifically whether the sampled frames adequately isolate key phases of the cardiac cycle, such as end-systole and end-diastole. Although EchoDino was pretrained on pediatric echocardiography, the external adult EchoNet-Dynamic test set provided a direct assessment of transfer across age groups. Using a fixed budget of $K=16$ uniformly sampled frames, EchoDino achieved mean absolute errors (MAEs) of 5.042, 4.800, and 6.410 on the adult EchoNet-Dynamic, EchoNet-Pediatric, and TCH-EF datasets, respectively (Table~\ref{tab:ef_prediction_unified}). These were the lowest MAEs among all uniformly sampled methods across the three cohorts, showing that EchoDino's frozen-frame features provide a strong foundation for temporal fusion in both pediatric and adult echocardiography.
