\begin{abstract}
Localization errors in estimating the position of a potential eavesdropper (Eve) pose a critical challenge to secure transmission in reconfigurable intelligent surface (RIS)-assisted integrated sensing and communication (ISAC) systems. To address this issue, this paper proposes a sensing-assisted robust secure transmission framework that exploits ISAC sensing to characterize the positioning uncertainty induced by these errors. The RIS is partitioned into a reflection region and a sensing reception region, supporting sensing and communication through spatial separation. By jointly exploiting observations collected at the base station (BS) and the RIS sensing reception region, maximum likelihood estimation (MLE) is employed to estimate the arrival angles, from which Eve's three-dimensional (3D) position is obtained through cross localization. The resulting localization Cramér–Rao lower bound (CRLB) is then used to construct an ellipsoidal uncertainty region around the estimated position. A two-stage optimization strategy is developed. In Stage I, the sensing beamformer and RIS reflection coefficients are jointly optimized to improve localization accuracy. In Stage II, the resulting positioning uncertainty is incorporated into a robust secure transmission design, where the communication beamformer and RIS reflection coefficients are jointly optimized to maximize the worst-case secrecy rate. Simulation results demonstrate that the proposed approach outperforms benchmark schemes without RIS assistance and with random RIS configurations in terms of secrecy performance. The proposed framework effectively mitigates secrecy performance degradation caused by localization errors in Eve's position.
\end{abstract}
\begin{IEEEkeywords}
RIS, ISAC, robust secure transmission, location uncertainty, CRLB.
\end{IEEEkeywords}
\section{Introduction}
With the continuous advancement of the sixth-generation (6G) wireless networks, future wireless systems are expected to evolve from conventional information transmission toward the integration of communication, sensing, computing, and intelligence~\cite{saad2020}. Integrated sensing and communication (ISAC) enables the sharing of wireless spectrum, radio-frequency (RF) hardware, and signal processing resources while simultaneously supporting high-rate data transmission and target detection, localization, and tracking~\cite{tan2021}. By sharing infrastructure, waveforms, and spectral resources, ISAC can improve the utilization efficiency of communication and sensing resources and reduce the hardware and spectrum overhead associated with conventionally separated systems~\cite{Liu2020,zhang2022}.
Therefore, ISAC has emerged as a fundamental framework for integrating communication and sensing capabilities in future wireless networks.

However, despite improving resource utilization, ISAC also introduces new security challenges. Since communication and sensing share wireless resources and signal waveforms, sensitive information may be exposed to interception by non-cooperative nodes. Meanwhile, the sensing capability of ISAC provides an opportunity to detect and localize potential eavesdroppers (Eves), linking sensing performance more closely to physical-layer security. In ISAC-enabled secure communication, Eve is often treated as a non-cooperative node whose exact location is difficult to obtain~\cite{Li2026Joint}. Moreover, practical sensing and channel estimation errors may cause beam misalignment and degrade secrecy performance~\cite{Matsumine2025Physical}. In particular, limited observations and measurement noise introduce uncertainty into Eve's estimated position~\cite{Hua2026Joint}, making it necessary to develop robust secure transmission schemes that explicitly account for localization errors.
Reconfigurable intelligent surfaces (RISs) provide additional spatial degrees of freedom for addressing these challenges. 
An RIS consists of a large number of low-cost and low-power programmable electromagnetic elements whose reflection coefficients can reshape the propagation direction and spatial distribution of wireless signals, thereby improving desired-signal quality, suppressing interference, and enhancing spatial coverage~\cite{huang2020,basar2020,bjornson2022}. 
RISs have been investigated in over-the-air federated learning, where RIS phase shifts are jointly optimized with receiver beamforming to facilitate the aggregation of distributed model updates over wireless channels~\cite{Xu2023EdgeLearning}.
For physical-layer security, RIS-assisted systems can jointly optimize BS active beamforming and RIS passive reflection coefficients to enhance the legitimate-user link while suppressing information leakage toward Eve~\cite{Li2026RIS}. 
The studies in~\cite{chen2020,cui2019} demonstrated that RISs could improve secure transmission performance, while~\cite{wu2021,feng2022} jointly optimized BS beamforming and RIS reflection coefficients to maximize the secrecy rate. 
In~\cite{Shen2023}, the additional spatial degrees of freedom provided by RISs were exploited through joint transmit beamforming and phase-shift optimization to mitigate eavesdropping threats under imperfect CSI of both legitimate and eavesdropping channels. 
Beyond communication enhancement, RISs can also reshape sensing propagation paths to strengthen echoes from non-cooperative targets and improve parameter estimation~\cite{Liu2024SNRCRB}. 
These capabilities make RISs a promising basis for integrating sensing and secure communication in ISAC systems.
In RIS-assisted ISAC systems, RISs have been investigated for sensing illumination, target echo reception, and communication reflection, enabling more integrated sensing and communication. The work in~\cite{liu2023ris} investigated the cooperative enhancement of RIS for sensing and communication, where the RIS reflected BS signals toward non-cooperative targets to enhance echoes and reconfigured its reflection phases to improve legitimate-user reception during communication. Furthermore,~\cite{Wu2021Tutorial,Zhang2024} further explored the application of simultaneously transmitting and reflecting (STAR)-RIS to joint sensing and communication design. By simultaneously controlling transmitted and reflected signals with flexible mode/time configurations, STAR-RISs provided additional spatial degrees of freedom, thereby enhancing the synergy between sensing and communication.
However, existing RIS-assisted secure communication studies either assumed perfect Eve CSI~\cite{Jain2025} or modeled Eve's spatial uncertainty over a predefined region~\cite{Salem2026}. These approaches did not explicitly characterize the location uncertainty arising from practical sensing errors, such as limited observations and estimation noise. To address sensing-related uncertainties, recent studies investigated sensing-assisted secure transmission~\cite{Su2024,Li2026Joint} and robust transmission under eavesdropper location uncertainty~\cite{Hong2026}. In addition, robust secure transmission with imperfect sensing estimates~\cite{Jiang2025RISAssistedIS} and joint time and beamforming optimization for RIS-assisted secure ISAC~\cite{Zhang2026JointTime} have also been studied. However, these two works adopted two-dimensional spatial models and therefore did not explicitly characterize Eve's full three-dimensional (3D) location uncertainty. More importantly, the explicit connection between sensing-derived localization accuracy and a CRLB-based 3D location uncertainty set, and its subsequent incorporation into robust RIS-assisted secure transmission design, remains insufficiently explored. These observations motivate a sensing-driven framework that translates localization accuracy into a 3D uncertainty set for Eve and incorporates this uncertainty into robust secure transmission optimization.
Motivated by the above discussion, this paper proposes a hybrid RIS-assisted secure ISAC system. The RIS panel is partitioned into a reflection region and a sensing reception region, and a two-stage time-division duplex (TDD) frame structure is adopted. During the sensing stage, the BS and the sensing reception region jointly collect the echoes from Eve and estimate its 3D position via bistatic localization. Based on the estimated position and its Cramér-Rao lower bound (CRLB), a location uncertainty ellipsoid is further constructed. During the communication stage, the BS beamformer and RIS reflection coefficients are jointly optimized based on the sensing-assisted location uncertainty set to maximize the worst-case secrecy rate, thereby establishing a sensing-to-communication design.
The main contributions of this paper are summarized as follows:
\begin{itemize}

	\item 
 A hybrid RIS-assisted ISAC secure transmission architecture is proposed, in which the RIS panel is partitioned into a reflection region and a sensing reception region to physically separate communication reflection from sensing reception. The 3D position of Eve is estimated from the joint observations collected by the BS and sensing reception region, and the corresponding localization CRLB is derived. Based on the CRLB, a location uncertainty ellipsoid associated with the sensing accuracy is further constructed, providing a sensing-assisted uncertainty model for robust secure transmission design.

	\item 
	A two-stage optimization framework is developed for sensing and secure transmission. In the sensing stage, the relationship between the CRLB and the equivalent illumination energy is exploited to formulate the sensing optimization, which is solved by jointly optimizing the sensing beamformer and RIS phase shifts using the closed-form maximum-ratio transmission (MRT) solution and Riemannian conjugate gradient (RCG) method. In the communication stage, the semi-infinite secrecy constraint under uncertain Eve location is transformed into a linear matrix inequality (LMI) by using first-order Taylor expansion and the S-lemma. The BS beamforming is optimized via semidefinite relaxation (SDR), while the RIS phase-shift subproblem is solved using a logarithmic barrier function and RCG, thereby achieving robust maximization of the worst-case secrecy rate. 

	\item 
	Numerical simulations are conducted to validate the proposed architecture and optimization methods. The relationship between the CRLB and location uncertainty ellipsoid is investigated, and the sensing optimization is shown to improve localization accuracy. The proposed scheme is compared with benchmarks with perfect Eve location information, without RIS, and with random RIS phases under different BS transmit powers and RIS sizes. The results demonstrate robust secrecy performance under Eve location uncertainty and reveal the tradeoff between sensing accuracy and robust secrecy performance.

\end{itemize}
The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. The system model is introduced in Section II. In Section III, the problem formulation is presented and the proposed optimization algorithm is developed. Numerical results are provided in Section IV, followed by the conclusion in Section V.
