	\label{fig2}
\end{figure}
As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig3}, the sensing time fraction $\tau$ introduces a tradeoff between localization accuracy and effective secrecy rate. For $P\leq30$~W, $R_{\rm eff}$ first increases and then decreases with $\tau$, reaching its peak at approximately $\tau^\star=0.10$--$0.15$. Increasing the sensing duration improves Eve's localization accuracy, thereby reducing the location uncertainty region and enhancing the worst-case secrecy performance. However, an excessively large $\tau$ reduces the available transmission time, causing the transmission-time loss to dominate. For $P=40$~W, $R_{\rm eff}$ generally decreases with $\tau$, indicating that the additional secrecy gain from sensing is insufficient to compensate for the loss in transmission time. Therefore, $\tau=0.15$ is adopted in the remaining simulations.
