	\caption{Convergence performance of the proposed alternating optimization algorithm.}
	\label{fig4}
\end{figure}
As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig5}, the worst-case secrecy rate increases with the BS transmit power for all antenna configurations, since higher transmit power improves the received signal quality at Bob. Moreover, increasing the BS antenna size leads to higher secrecy rates. For a small-scale array, e.g., a $2\times2$ UPA, the performance gain is limited by the insufficient spatial degrees of freedom for simultaneously enhancing Bob's signal and suppressing information leakage toward Eve under location uncertainty. With more antennas, the increased array gain and spatial degrees of freedom enable more effective beamforming optimization, resulting in higher worst-case secrecy rates. These results highlight the importance of the BS spatial degrees of freedom in robust RIS-assisted ISAC secure transmission.
