\section{Conclusion}
This paper has investigated robust secure transmission in RIS-assisted ISAC systems with Eve location uncertainty and proposed a sensing-assisted two-stage optimization framework. In the first stage, the sensing beamformer and RIS reflection coefficients have been jointly optimized for accurate Eve localization, based on which an ellipsoidal location uncertainty region has been constructed using the localization CRLB. In the second stage, the communication beamformer and RIS reflection coefficients have been jointly optimized to maximize the worst-case secrecy rate, where the robust constraints have been reformulated into tractable LMI forms. Simulation results have demonstrated the effectiveness of the proposed approach and revealed a tradeoff between the sensing time fraction and the effective secrecy rate. Moreover, increasing the BS antennas, transmit power, and RIS elements can improve the worst-case secrecy rate, while the proposed method outperforms the benchmark schemes. Future work will extend the proposed framework to more practical and complex scenarios, including multiple legitimate users, multiple non-colluding eavesdroppers, and multiple RISs.
