}
\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
Rotatable antennas (RAs) can improve wireless link performance by adjusting their boresight directions. 
However, existing RA studies generally assume instantaneous antenna reconfiguration, whereas mechanical rotation incurs non-negligible delay in practical deployment.
To address this issue, we develop a rotation-delay-aware communication model for finite communication slots, where mechanical rotation directly reduces the time available for data transmission. This model reveals a fundamental tradeoff between the communication gain achieved through RA reorientation and the associated transmission-time loss. Accordingly, we formulate a rotation-delay-aware joint design of RA orientations and transmit beamforming to maximize the minimum effective transmission rate.
For the single-user system, under a sufficient condition for strict concavity, the global optimum falls into one of three regimes: no rotation, partial rotation, and full rotation.
A closed-form approximation is derived using a second-order expansion. 
For the multi-user system, we separate rotation-budget selection from fixed-budget max-min signal-to-interference-plus-noise ratio (SINR) optimization.
An analytic-gradient zero-forcing (AG-ZF) algorithm is then developed for low-complexity joint RA orientation and rotation-budget optimization.
The results show that explicitly accounting for mechanical rotation delay is essential for finite-slot RA-enabled UAV communications, and that the proposed designs effectively balance reconfiguration gain and transmission-time loss while achieving favorable effective-rate performance with low computational complexity.
\end{abstract}
\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Antenna orientation, rotatable antenna, rotation delay, UAV downlink communications, zero-forcing beamforming.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}
\IEEEPARstart{U}{nmanned} aerial vehicles (UAVs) have been widely used in inspection, logistics, mapping, emergency communications, and cooperative sensing due to their high mobility, flexible deployment, and three-dimensional coverage capabilities~\cite{ZengYongUAV,HayatUAV,MozaffariUAV,ZengUAVTutorial}. 
In UAV downlink communications, a mobile UAV can serve multiple spatially distributed users~\cite{XuUAV}. As the UAV moves, the relative geometry and propagation directions of the links between the UAV and the users can change over time. 
In mission-driven applications, UAV trajectories may be predetermined~\cite{PredeterminedTrajectory}, motivating antenna-side adaptation without altering the flight path~\cite{GuoBeamforming}.
Conventional antenna arrays can adapt their beamforming weights to the instantaneous channel~\cite{Khallilibeamforming,Lyubeamforming}, but their element positions and orientations remain fixed, limiting their spatial reconfiguration capability.
When users are located in low-gain antenna directions or their channels are highly correlated, beamforming alone may provide limited performance improvement. 
To improve channel adaptability, several reconfigurable antenna architectures have recently been investigated, including movable antennas (MAs), six-dimensional movable antennas (6DMAs), and rotatable antennas (RAs)~\cite{MA3D,HuaHaocheng6DMA,ZhengBeixiongRA,ZhengBeixiongRASurvey,PeiFengchengOptRA,RAModel,XiangLinEnergy}. 
Such architectures can reconfigure the wireless channel by adjusting antenna positions or orientations. 
They can improve link gains and reduce inter-user channel correlation.
Among these architectures, RAs adjust the boresight directions of antenna elements while keeping their physical positions fixed. 
Hence, RAs can change the effective antenna gains toward different propagation directions without requiring additional space for antenna translation. 
This feature makes RAs attractive for size, weight, and power (SWaP)-constrained UAV platforms~\cite{ZhangXuzhongRAUAV,RAUAV,RAUAV3,RAUAV2}.
RA reconfiguration can be implemented using mechanical or electronic mechanisms. Electronic RAs offer fast reconfiguration, but their achievable steering states are generally constrained by the underlying tunable hardware~\cite{Chiao1999MEMS,ZhengBeixiongTutorial}. In contrast, mechanical RAs directly rotate the physical antenna boresight and provide continuous wide-angle orientation control~\cite{ZhengBeixiongTutorial}. Such flexibility is well suited to UAV downlink communications with spatially distributed users and time-varying link directions, enabling directional antenna gains to be more effectively exploited through boresight adjustment. Therefore, this work focuses on mechanically driven RAs.
Most existing RA studies optimize communication performance after antenna reconfiguration without explicitly accounting for the execution time of rotation~\cite{ZhengBeixiongRA,PeiFengchengOptRA,ZhangXuzhongRAUAV,RAModel,PeiFengchengRA}.
Recent work has further considered finite rotation-speed constraints and the energy consumption of dynamic mechanical steering~\cite{XiangLinEnergy}. However, these studies do not explicitly model the reduction in available data-transmission time caused by mechanical rotation within a finite communication slot.
Related work on movable antennas (MAs) has incorporated mechanical movement delay into finite transmission blocks~\cite{WangHonghaoMA,LiQingliangMA,ZhengXiMA}, but the resulting delay-aware designs are based on antenna repositioning and are not directly applicable to RA systems.
Mechanical RA reorientation incurs non-negligible delay due to finite rotation speed~\cite{ZhengBeixiongTutorial,ViewproTraceG1}, reducing the time available for data transmission within a finite communication slot.
Hence, RA reconfiguration involves a tradeoff between communication improvement and transmission-time loss.
Optimizing only the communication performance after reconfiguration can be suboptimal when rotation time is included in the slot.
This motivates explicit consideration of mechanical rotation delay in finite-slot RA communications. Incorporating rotation delay, however, makes the RA design more involved. Since all RAs rotate in parallel, the reconfiguration delay is determined by the maximum rotation angle, which couples the orientation decisions of different antennas through a common loss of transmission time. Meanwhile, the RA orientations determine the directional channel gains and hence the multi-user channel matrix, making them strongly coupled with transmit beamforming and inter-user interference. As a result, the rotation budget, RA orientations, and transmit beamforming are tightly coupled in the original formulation, leading to a challenging nonconvex optimization problem.
Building on the above discussion, we study an RA-enabled UAV downlink communication system with mechanical rotation delay under finite communication slots, as illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig场景图}. 
Unlike existing RA models that neglect reconfiguration delay, we explicitly account for the time consumed by antenna rotation. 
The main contributions are summarized as follows.
