	\centering
	\includegraphics[
		width=0.9\textwidth,
		clip
	]{figure/Scenario.pdf}
	\caption{Illustration of the considered RA-enabled UAV downlink communication scenario.}
	\label{fig场景图}
\end{figure*}
\begin{itemize}
 \item We first develop a rotation-delay-aware communication model for mechanically driven RAs under finite communication slots.
 With parallel antenna rotation, the reconfiguration delay is determined by the maximum rotation angle among all RAs.
 Based on this model, we define the effective transmission rate and formulate a joint optimization problem for RA orientations, transmit beamforming, and the rotation budget.

 \item For the single-user system, we reduce the rotation-delay-aware joint design to a one-dimensional rotation problem by exploiting the optimal beamforming and fixed-budget RA pointing structures.
 Under a sufficient condition for strict concavity, the global optimum is unique and falls into three regimes: no rotation, partial rotation, and full rotation.
 We further derive a closed-form approximation through a second-order expansion, whose effective-rate performance is evaluated numerically.

 \item For the multi-user system, we reveal a separable structure in the rotation-delay-aware joint problem.

 The problem can be equivalently decomposed into an outer optimization over the common rotation budget and a fixed-budget max-min signal-to-interference-plus-noise ratio (SINR) optimization.

 For a given rotation budget, we impose a zero-forcing (ZF) structure so that the
 beamforming directions are determined by the RA orientations, while the
 remaining max-min fair power allocation is obtained in closed form,
 reducing the fixed-budget design to a Gram-matrix-based RA pointing problem.
 Building on this structure, we derive analytical gradients and spherical-cap feasible updates, leading to the proposed low-complexity analytic-gradient zero-forcing (AG-ZF) algorithm.

 \item Numerical results verify the importance of accounting for mechanical rotation delay in finite-slot UAV communications.
 The closed-form design achieves near-optimal effective-rate performance, while AG-ZF achieves favorable effective-rate performance across different multi-user settings.
 Theoretical complexity analysis further demonstrates the computational advantage of AG-ZF.
\end{itemize}
The remainder of this paper is organized as follows.
Section II presents the system model and defines the effective transmission rate with mechanical rotation delay.
Section III studies the single-user system, characterizes the optimal rotation structure, and derives an approximate closed-form solution.
Section IV considers the multi-user system, develops the AG-ZF algorithm, and analyzes its computational complexity.
Section V presents numerical results to evaluate the proposed rotation-delay-aware design and optimization algorithms.
Finally, Section VI concludes the paper.
