\section{Conclusion}
This paper incorporated mechanical rotation delay into RA-enabled UAV downlink communications and jointly optimized RA orientations and transmit beamforming to maximize the minimum effective transmission rate under finite communication slots. 
The results show that accounting for rotation delay is necessary to maximize the effective transmission rate.
For the single-user system, we characterized three optimal rotation regimes, no rotation, partial rotation, and full rotation, under a sufficient condition for strict concavity. 
A closed-form approximation of the optimal rotation budget was further derived using a second-order expansion. 
For the multi-user system, a ZF-based formulation was developed to reduce the fixed-budget design to a Gram-matrix-based RA orientation problem with max-min fair power allocation.
Based on the resulting formulation, the AG-ZF algorithm was developed to efficiently solve the problem. 
Simulation results demonstrated the near-optimal effective-rate performance of the single-user closed-form approximation and the performance advantages of AG-ZF over the considered benchmark schemes. 
Future work may consider inter-slot RA reconfiguration under UAV mobility and incorporate practical factors such as rotation acceleration, settling time, mechanical energy consumption, and imperfect channel state information.
