\section{Conclusion}
We present a plug-in framework for concept-based interpretability and refinement of off-the-shelf medical deep learning models \cite{koh2020concept}. By linearly mapping a pre-trained encoder to the BioMedCLIP embedding space, our approach constructs an intervenable Concept Bottleneck Model (CBM) without architectural modifications. Beyond passive explanation, gradient-guided concept interventions enable domain experts to perform counterfactual debugging and targeted classification head fine-tuning, directly correcting systematic failure modes to enhance trust in clinical deployments.
A key limitation is the spatial and structural disconnect between global clinical narratives and specific image frames. While rich semantic concepts are natively documented within radiology notes at the time of diagnosis, these clinical summaries describe entire multi-view studies globally and lack granular, instance-level association with individual images or slices. Consequently, rather than relying on uncalibrated, off-the-shelf foundation spaces, future research will focus on developing weakly-supervised alignment techniques to dynamically map text-mined concepts from native clinical reports directly to corresponding image volumes \cite{bhalla2024interpreting}, mitigating data alignment bottlenecks across diverse clinical environments.
\paragraph{\textbf{Prospects of Application:}} This framework can be deployed as an auditing plug-in for legacy clinical workflows. In interactive environments, it enables real-time, human-in-the-loop decision debugging, allowing clinicians to dynamically verify model reasoning, suppress misleading artifacts, and safely refine diagnostic heads at the point of care without end-to-end retraining.
